OAI_PAPER({"slug": "A-Three-Dimensional-Counterexample-to-Integer-Degree-Harmonic-Dimension-Comparison-September-26-2026", "title": "A Three-Dimensional Counterexample to Integer-Degree Harmonic Dimension Comparison", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/A-Three-Dimensional-Counterexample-to-Integer-Degree-Harmonic-Dimension-Comparison-September-26-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "361", "family_title": "Failure of integer-degree harmonic dimension comparison", "abstract_html": "For every \\(4/9\\lt v\\lt 1\\) and \\(1\\lt c\\lt 9v/4\\), all sufficiently large integers <i>k</i> admit a complete smooth metric on ℝ<sup>3</sup> with nonnegative Ricci curvature, asymptotic volume ratio <i>v</i>, and at least \\(c(k+1)^2\\) linearly independent real harmonic functions of pointwise growth at most <i>k</i>. This answers Yau's integer-degree dimension comparison question negatively in dimension three, with a fixed-factor excess over the Euclidean count. For each \\(1\\lt c\\lt 9/4\\), these metrics can be chosen arbitrarily close to the Euclidean metric in global bi-Lipschitz distance. The metric may depend on <i>k</i>.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem \\(1.1\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is an integer \\(k_0(v,c)\\) such that every integer \\(k\\geq k_0(v,c)\\) admits a complete smooth metric \\(g_k\\) on \\(\\mathbb R^3\\), Euclidean near the origin, with …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For a connected complete smooth Riemannian manifold \\((M,g)\\) without boundary and \\(d\\geq 0\\), let \\(\\mathcal H_d(M,g)\\) be the real vector space of smooth harmonic functions satisfying\n  \\[\n  |u(x)|\\leq C_u(1+d_g(o,x))^d\\qquad(x\\in M)\n  \\]\n  for some finite constant \\(C_u\\). The space is independent of the base point \\(o\\). Write \\(h_d(M,g)=\\dim_{\\mathbb R}\\mathcal H_d(M,g)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(g_E\\) be the Euclidean metric on \\(\\mathbb R^3\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Normalize asymptotic volume ratio by\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{AVR}(g)=\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\n  \\frac{\\operatorname{vol}_g B_g(o,R)}{(4\\pi/3)R^3}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A pointed tangent cone at infinity is a pointed Gromov–Hausdorff limit of \\((M,R_j^{-2}g,o)\\) along \\(R_j\\to\\infty\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(v\\in(4/9,1)\\) and \\(c\\in(1,9v/4)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an integer \\(k_0(v,c)\\) such that every integer \\(k\\geq k_0(v,c)\\) admits a complete smooth metric \\(g_k\\) on \\(\\mathbb R^3\\), Euclidean near the origin, with\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ric}_{g_k}\\geq0,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{AVR}(g_k)=v,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nh_k(\\mathbb R^3,g_k)\\geq c(k+1)^2&gt;(k+1)^2\n=h_k(\\mathbb R^3,g_E).\n\\]\nThe Ricci curvature is positive outside a compact set. The metric has uncountably many pairwise nonisometric pointed tangent cones at infinity. There are points tending to infinity and planes containing the radial direction at those points whose sectional curvature is negative.</p><p>The metric is chosen after \\(k\\) and may depend on \\(k\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>First, the Euclidean comparison space consists exactly of harmonic polynomials of degree at most \\(k\\). A smooth radial averaging kernel at scale \\(R\\) reproduces a Euclidean harmonic function. Moving any \\(k+1\\) derivatives onto that kernel bounds the derivative at a fixed point by \\(O(R^{-1})\\) under degree-\\(k\\) growth. Letting \\(R\\to\\infty\\) makes all such derivatives zero. Each homogeneous part of the resulting polynomial is harmonic.</p><p>In three variables, the dimension of homogeneous harmonic polynomials of degree \\(l\\) is\n\\[\n\\binom{l+2}{2}-\\binom l2=2l+1,\n\\]\nwith the second term zero for \\(l&lt;2\\). The polynomial Laplacian onto degree \\(l-2\\) is surjective because its adjoint in the monomial inner product\n\\[\n\\langle x^\\alpha,x^\\alpha\\rangle=\\alpha!\n\\]\nis multiplication by \\(|x|^2\\), which is injective. Thus\n\\[\nh_k(\\mathbb R^3,g_E)=\\sum_{l=0}^k(2l+1)=(k+1)^2.\n\\]</p><p>Given \\(4/9&lt;v&lt;1\\) and \\(1&lt;c&lt;9v/4\\), set\n\\[\na=\\sqrt v,\\qquad\n\\sqrt c&lt;\\vartheta&lt;\\frac{3a}{2},\n\\]\nand choose \\(a^2&lt;\\kappa_*&lt;1\\).</p><p>Fix the equatorial bump and a sufficiently small convex near-round neighborhood. It may be arbitrarily small before \\(k\\) is chosen; in particular impose the strict frequency bound\n\\[\n\\frac{2\\vartheta}{3a}\\sqrt{C_*}&lt;1\n\\]\nused in Proposition \\(1\\). The angular construction imposes only cofinite conditions on \\(k\\). Choose one threshold above all of them and large enough that\n\\[\n(\\lfloor\\vartheta k\\rfloor+1)^2\\geq c(k+1)^2.\n\\]</p><p>For each integer above this threshold, choose its finite angular program, crossing directions, gaps, and disjoint sign neighborhoods using Proposition \\(1\\). Choose the Ricci buffer \\(C\\) next. Proposition \\(2\\) then works for every sufficiently large \\(J\\). Increase \\(J\\) to satisfy the freezing-slab, relative graph, coordinate-box, and endpoint-sign requirements of Propositions \\(4\\)–\\(7\\). None imposes an upper bound on \\(J\\).</p><p>Proposition \\(7\\) selects one control sequence common to all \\(p\\) lines. Proposition \\(8\\) supplies\n\\[\np=(\\lfloor\\vartheta k\\rfloor+1)^2\n\\geq c(k+1)^2\n\\]\nindependent entire functions in \\(\\mathcal H_k\\) for its single metric \\(g_k\\).</p><p>Proposition \\(2\\) gives smoothness, completeness, the Euclidean core, nonnegative Ricci curvature, and positive Ricci curvature outside a compact set. Proposition \\(3\\) gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{AVR}(g_k)=a^2=v,\n\\]\nuncountably many nonisometric pointed tangent cones, and negative radial sectional planes along a sequence tending to infinity. These properties hold for every provisional control sequence and hence for the selected one.</p><p>Thus one threshold is chosen first, then each eligible integer \\(k\\), then its metric \\(g_k\\), exactly as claimed. No common metric for all degrees is asserted.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Corollary \\(1.2\\)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For all sufficiently large integers \\(k\\), there are metrics \\(g_k\\) with the conclusions of Theorem \\(1\\) and \\( (1+\\epsilon)^{-2}g_E\\leq g_k\\leq(1+\\epsilon)^2g_E \\qquad\\text{on }\\mathbb R^3. \\) Their asymptotic volume …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the harmonic-growth spaces, asymptotic volume ratio, and tangent-cone convention of Theorem \\(1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\) and \\(1&lt;c&lt;9/4\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For all sufficiently large integers \\(k\\), there are metrics \\(g_k\\) with the conclusions of Theorem \\(1\\) and\n\\[\n(1+\\epsilon)^{-2}g_E\\leq g_k\\leq(1+\\epsilon)^2g_E\n\\qquad\\text{on }\\mathbb R^3.\n\\]\nTheir asymptotic volume ratio can be prescribed arbitrarily close to \\(1\\). In particular, the identity map is globally \\((1+\\epsilon)\\)-bi-Lipschitz.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\) and \\(1&lt;c&lt;9/4\\). Choose\n\\[\n\\max\\left\\{\\frac{2\\sqrt c}{3},(1+\\epsilon)^{-1}\\right\\}&lt;a&lt;1.\n\\]\nChoose \\(0&lt;\\delta&lt;1\\) small enough that\n\\[\na^2(1-\\delta)\\geq(1+\\epsilon)^{-2},\n\\qquad\n1+\\delta\\leq(1+\\epsilon)^2.\n\\]</p><p>Run the construction in the proof of Theorem \\(1\\) with \\(v=a^2\\), taking the initial conformal neighborhood sufficiently small for the angular comparison in Lemma \\(1.1\\) with this \\(\\delta\\). Choose the reference program and interpolation with positive margin inside that neighborhood, so all controls remain there once \\(J\\) is large.</p><p>The global comparison in Proposition \\(2\\) gives\n\\[\n(1+\\epsilon)^{-2}g_E\\leq g_k\\leq(1+\\epsilon)^2g_E.\n\\]\nIntegrating along curves and taking infima gives the stated global bi-Lipschitz bounds for the identity.</p><p>The number \\(a\\) may be chosen arbitrarily close to \\(1\\); hence the prescribed asymptotic volume ratio \\(a^2\\) may also be arbitrarily close to \\(1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every sufficiently large integer \\(k\\), with threshold allowed to depend on \\(\\mathcal U\\), there are \\(S>0\\) and a smooth \\(S\\)-periodic path \\(w(s)\\in\\mathcal U\\), with \\(H(s)=h(w(s))\\), satisfying the following …", "statement": "<p>**Angular program; original Proposition \\(3.1\\).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(g_0\\) be the unit round metric on \\(S^2\\), and put\n  \\[\n  d\\mu=\\frac{d\\operatorname{vol}_{g_0}}{4\\pi}.\n  \\]\n  Use the nonpositive convention for the Laplacian. Eigenvalues of \\(-\\Delta_h\\), repeated according to multiplicity, are numbered from \\(1\\), with \\(\\lambda_1(h)=0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  B_l=(l+1)^2,\\qquad B_{-1}=0.\n  \\]\n  The round degree-\\(l\\) eigenspace \\(\\mathcal Y_l\\) has eigenvalue \\(l(l+1)\\), dimension \\(2l+1\\), and occupies positions \\(B_{l-1}+1,\\ldots,B_l\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(w\\in C^\\infty(S^2;\\mathbb R)\\), define\n  \\[\n  A(w)=\\int_{S^2}e^{2w}\\,d\\mu,\\qquad\n  \\rho_w=\\frac{e^{2w}}{A(w)},\\qquad\n  h^0(w)=\\rho_wg_0,\\qquad d\\mu_w=\\rho_wd\\mu.\n  \\]\n  Let \\(h(w)=\\Phi_w^*h^0(w)\\) be the fixed-area coordinate gauge constructed in Lemma \\(1.1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Work in a sufficiently small convex \\(C^2\\)-neighborhood \\(\\mathcal U\\) of \\(0\\), among smooth functions. Every path and perturbation used below belongs to a finite-dimensional smooth family contained in \\(\\mathcal U\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix\n  \\[\n  \\frac23&lt;a&lt;1,\\qquad 1&lt;\\vartheta&lt;\\frac{3a}{2},\\qquad a^2&lt;\\kappa_*&lt;1,\n  \\]\n  and require \\(K_{h^0(w)}&gt;\\kappa_*\\) on \\(\\mathcal U\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For an integer \\(k\\), set\n  \\[\n  M=\\lfloor\\vartheta k\\rfloor,\\quad L=\\lfloor\\sqrt{k}\\rfloor,\\quad\n  p=B_M=(M+1)^2,\\quad\n  I=\\{B_L+1,\\ldots,p\\},\\quad N=p-B_L.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  d(x)=\\frac{-1+\\sqrt{1+4x}}2.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every sufficiently large integer \\(k\\), with threshold allowed to depend on \\(\\mathcal U\\), there are \\(S&gt;0\\) and a smooth \\(S\\)-periodic path \\(w(s)\\in\\mathcal U\\), with \\(H(s)=h(w(s))\\), satisfying the following properties.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. The phase origin can be chosen so that \\(w(s)=w(0)\\) for \\(|s|&lt;\\epsilon_0\\), for some \\(\\epsilon_0&gt;0\\), and \\(H(0)\\) is simple through position \\(p+1\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Pointwise,\n   \\[\n   d\\operatorname{vol}_{H(s)}=4\\pi d\\mu,\n   \\qquad \\lambda_p(H(s))&lt;\\lambda_{p+1}(H(s))\n   \\quad\\text{for every }s.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. On the unwrapped phase line there is a smooth real \\(L^2(d\\mu)\\)-orthonormal frame \\(e_1(s),\\ldots,e_p(s)\\) of continued eigenlines, with \\(e_1=1\\). Their eigenvalues, denoted by \\(\\lambda_i(s)\\), have only isolated double crossings at distinct phases. At each crossing, the traceless first derivative of its \\(2\\times2\\) cluster matrix is nonzero.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. The return permutation after \\(S\\) fixes positions \\(1,\\ldots,B_L\\) and is one cycle on \\(I\\). The number \\(NS\\) is an eigenvalue period for every label, and\n   \\[\n   \\frac1{NS}\\int_0^{NS}d(a^{-2}\\lambda_i(s))\\,ds&lt;k\n   \\qquad(1\\leq i\\leq p).\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">5. At every crossing of labels \\(i,h\\), there is a smooth conformal direction \\(\\xi\\) such that\n   \\[\n   \\left\\langle e_h,\n   \\left.\\frac{d}{d\\varepsilon}\\right|_{\\varepsilon=0}\n   (-\\Delta_{h(w+\\varepsilon\\xi)})e_i\n   \\right\\rangle_{L^2(d\\mu)}\\neq0.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">6. Once \\(k\\) and the program are fixed, the outer gap, absolute crossing slopes, and phase separations have positive uniform lower bounds. Every fixed smooth norm of the frame and metrics has a uniform upper bound. Each pair has a positive eigenvalue gap on every compact subset of \\(\\mathbb R/(NS\\mathbb Z)\\) disjoint from its crossing set; a pair with no crossings has a uniform gap on that whole circle.</p>", "proof": "<p>Shrink \\(\\mathcal U\\), if necessary, so mass-form min–max gives\n\\[\n\\lambda_j(h(w))\\leq C_*l(l+1)\n\\quad\\text{when }B_{l-1}&lt;j\\leq B_l,\n\\qquad\n\\frac{2\\vartheta}{3a}\\sqrt{C_*}&lt;1.\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\nThis is possible because \\(C_*\\to1\\) as the neighborhood shrinks and \\(\\vartheta&lt;3a/2\\). Lemma \\(1.6\\) applies for all sufficiently large \\(k\\).</p><p>At each designated double choose a conformal direction with nonzero traceless first derivative, using Lemma \\(1.2\\). On a short path through the double, let its matrix be \\(M(\\tau)\\), \\(M(0)=\\lambda I\\). The quotient\n\\[\n\\widehat M(\\tau)\n=\\frac{M(\\tau)-\\frac12\\operatorname{tr}M(\\tau)I}{\\tau}\n\\]\nextends smoothly across zero and is a nonzero trace-free symmetric matrix there. Its two eigenvalues are distinct. Its eigenlines, and hence those of \\(M(\\tau)\\), extend smoothly, with eigenvalues\n\\[\n\\frac12\\operatorname{tr}M(\\tau)+\\tau\\nu_+(\\tau),\n\\qquad\n\\frac12\\operatorname{tr}M(\\tau)+\\tau\\nu_-(\\tau),\n\\qquad \\nu_+(0)&gt;\\nu_-(0).\n\\]\nTheir order reverses at zero, exchanging precisely the designated adjacent positions with a simple eigenvalue-difference zero.</p><p>Choose one base parameter simple through \\(p+1\\). By Lemma \\(1.3\\), join the two endpoints of each short crossing segment to that base by simple paths. Each resulting loop has only its designated double through \\(p+1\\). Reparametrize smoothly to be constant near joins and the base, while retaining nonzero speed at the crossing. All designated pairs lie in \\(I\\), so the outer gap remains open.</p><p>Concatenate these loops in the order\n\\[\ni=B_L+1,B_L+2,\\ldots,p-1.\n\\]\nThe return permutation \\(C\\) satisfies\n\\[\nC(B_L+1)=p,\\qquad C(j)=j-1\n\\quad(B_L+2\\leq j\\leq p).\n\\]\nIt is one \\(N\\)-cycle on \\(I\\), fixing all lower positions. Extend the parameter loop \\(S\\)-periodically, with the origin in a constant base interval. Lemma \\(1.1\\) makes its fixed-area image periodic.</p><p>Simple eigenlines extend along simple portions and through doubles by the quotient construction. Choose a smooth orthonormal real frame on the unwrapped line, with \\(e_1=1\\). After \\(N\\) periods each band line returns to itself. Unit sections may acquire signs; after at most \\(2NS\\) the chosen frame repeats. Signs affect neither eigenvalues nor positions.</p><p>There are finitely many crossing types per label period. Compactness gives the asserted uniform gaps, slopes, separations, and smooth bounds. Every pair of band labels crosses during a label period: the label entering at \\(B_L+1\\) crosses all other band labels and ends at \\(p\\), and every label takes that entering position in one of the \\(N\\) repetitions. Lower labels never cross. Lemma \\(1.2\\) supplies the stated off-diagonal direction at each double, and finite phase separation permits later localization near individual crossings.</p><p>Since \\(d(x)\\leq\\sqrt x\\), at an ordered position belonging to the round degree-\\(l\\) block,\n\\[\nd(a^{-2}\\lambda_j(H(s)))\n&lt;a^{-1}\\sqrt{C_*}(l+1).\n\\]\nA fixed lower label therefore has frequency \\(O(\\sqrt k)&lt;k\\).</p><p>For a band label \\(i\\), let \\(\\sigma_t\\) send a base rank to its ordered rank at a noncrossing phase \\(t\\in[0,S]\\). In repetition \\(n\\), its rank is \\(\\sigma_tC^n(i)\\). For \\(0\\leq n&lt;N\\), these run once through \\(I\\). The finite crossing set does not affect integration, so\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac1{NS}\\int_0^{NS}d(a^{-2}\\lambda_i(s))\\,ds\n&amp;=\\frac1{NS}\\int_0^S\\sum_{j\\in I}\nd(a^{-2}\\lambda_j(H(t)))\\,dt\\\\\n&amp;\\leq\na^{-1}\\sqrt{C_*}\\,\n\\frac{\\sum_{l=L+1}^M(2l+1)(l+1)}\n{(M+1)^2-(L+1)^2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe numerator is\n\\[\n\\sum_{t=L+2}^{M+1}(2t^2-t)\n=\\frac23\\bigl((M+1)^3-(L+1)^3\\bigr)+O(M^2+L^2).\n\\]\nSince \\(L/k\\to0\\), \\(M/k\\to\\vartheta\\), the last bound divided by \\(k\\) tends to\n\\[\n\\frac{2\\vartheta\\sqrt{C_*}}{3a}&lt;1.\n\\]\nIt is therefore strictly below \\(k\\) for all sufficiently large \\(k\\).</p><p>Fix \\(k\\) and its entire finite angular program now. The maximum of the finitely many means remains strictly below \\(k\\). Every gap, slope, direction, and smooth bound is fixed before the later radial start is chosen.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The construction is well-defined and smooth in every additional smooth finite parameter, and \\( d\\operatorname{vol}_{h(w)}=d\\operatorname{vol}_{g_0}=4\\pi d\\mu. \\) It depends only on \\(w\\), with …", "statement": "<p>**Fixed angular measure and near-round control; original unnumbered construction in Section \\(2.1\\).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(g_0,\\mu,\\rho_w,h^0(w)\\), and \\(\\mathcal U\\) from Proposition \\(1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(0\\leq\\tau\\leq1\\), set\n  \\[\n  \\rho_{\\tau,w}=1+\\tau(\\rho_w-1).\n  \\]\n  Let \\(\\phi_{\\tau,w}\\) be the mean-zero solution of\n  \\[\n  \\Delta_0\\phi_{\\tau,w}=-\\partial_\\tau\\rho_{\\tau,w},\n  \\]\n  and set \\(X_{\\tau,w}=\\rho_{\\tau,w}^{-1}\\nabla_0\\phi_{\\tau,w}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Phi_{\\tau,w}\\) be its flow beginning at the identity, and put\n  \\[\n  \\Phi_w=\\Phi_{1,w},\\qquad h(w)=\\Phi_w^*h^0(w).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The construction is well-defined and smooth in every additional smooth finite parameter, and\n\\[\nd\\operatorname{vol}_{h(w)}=d\\operatorname{vol}_{g_0}=4\\pi d\\mu.\n\\]\nIt depends only on \\(w\\), with \\(\\Phi_0=\\operatorname{id}\\), so a closed parameter path gives a closed metric path. It preserves spectra and Gauss curvature. For antipodally even \\(w\\), both \\(h^0(w)\\) and \\(h(w)\\) preserve the even and odd function spaces.</p><p>For every \\(0&lt;\\delta&lt;1\\), a sufficiently small convex \\(C^2\\)-neighborhood \\(\\mathcal U\\) ensures\n\\[\n(1-\\delta)g_0\\leq h(w)\\leq(1+\\delta)g_0\n\\qquad(w\\in\\mathcal U).\n\\]\nOn each compact finite-parameter family, the gauged metrics have bounded smooth geometry.</p>", "proof": "<p>The function \\(\\rho_{\\tau,w}\\) is positive and has integral \\(1\\). The Poisson right-hand side has mean zero, so its mean-zero solution exists uniquely. Moser’s volume-form calculation gives\n\\[\n\\frac{d}{d\\tau}\\Phi_{\\tau,w}^*(\\rho_{\\tau,w}d\\mu)\n=\n\\Phi_{\\tau,w}^*\n\\bigl[\\partial_\\tau\\rho_{\\tau,w}\n+\\operatorname{div}_0(\\rho_{\\tau,w}X_{\\tau,w})\\bigr]d\\mu=0.\n\\]\nHence \\(\\Phi_w^*(\\rho_wd\\mu)=d\\mu\\), proving the fixed-area assertion.</p><p>The Poisson solution and flow depend smoothly on additional finite parameters. The construction depends only on \\(w\\), and \\(\\Phi_0=\\operatorname{id}\\). Pullback preserves spectrum and curvature. If \\(w\\) is antipodally even, the Poisson solution is even, its vector field is equivariant under the antipodal map, and its flow commutes with that map.</p><p>For the quantitative comparison, fix \\(0&lt;\\alpha&lt;1\\), and write \\(\\sigma=\\|w\\|_{C^2}\\). For small \\(\\sigma\\), normalization gives\n\\[\n\\|\\rho_w-1\\|_{C^1}+\\|\\rho_w-1\\|_{C^{0,\\alpha}}\\leq C\\sigma.\n\\]\nThe Poisson solution is independent of \\(\\tau\\), since its right-hand side is \\(-(\\rho_w-1)\\). The elliptic estimate on the fixed round sphere bounds its \\(C^{2,\\alpha}\\)-norm by \\(C\\sigma\\), and consequently\n\\[\n\\|X_{\\tau,w}\\|_{C^1}\\leq C\\sigma\n\\]\nuniformly in \\(\\tau\\). The variational equation for the flow gives\n\\[\ne^{-C\\sigma}g_0\\leq\\Phi_w^*g_0\\leq e^{C\\sigma}g_0.\n\\]\nMultiplication by \\(\\rho_w\\circ\\Phi_w\\) gives the same bounds for \\(h(w)\\), after increasing \\(C\\). Shrinking \\(\\mathcal U\\) gives the stated tensor comparison uniformly over finite-parameter families.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "As the smooth conformal direction varies, the traceless compression of the Laplacian’s first variation to \\(\\operatorname{span}\\{u,v\\}\\) has rank two.", "statement": "<p>**Two independent splitting directions; original Lemma \\(2.1\\).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the conformal metrics and fixed-area gauge of Proposition \\(1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\lambda&gt;0\\) be an eigenvalue of \\(h^0(w)\\), and let \\(u,v\\) be an orthonormal pair in its real eigenspace for \\(L^2(d\\mu_w)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>As the smooth conformal direction varies, the traceless compression of the Laplacian’s first variation to \\(\\operatorname{span}\\{u,v\\}\\) has rank two.</p><p>If \\(w\\) is antipodally even and \\(u,v\\) have the same antipodal parity, the conclusion remains valid using only even conformal directions.</p><p>The same splitting matrices apply after the fixed-area coordinate change.</p>", "proof": "<p>In dimension two the normalized Dirichlet form is\n\\[\nq(f,g)=\\int_{S^2}\\langle\\nabla_0f,\\nabla_0g\\rangle\\,d\\mu,\n\\]\nindependent of \\(w\\). Its mass form is\n\\[\nm_w(f,g)=\\int_{S^2}fg\\,d\\mu_w.\n\\]\nDifferentiation gives\n\\[\n\\dot m_w(f,g)\n=\n2\\int_{S^2}fg\n\\left(\\dot w-\\int_{S^2}\\dot w\\,d\\mu_w\\right)d\\mu_w.\n\\]\nFor an \\(m_w\\)-orthonormal basis \\(u_1,\\ldots,u_r\\) of a repeated positive eigenspace, differentiation of \\(q=\\lambda m_w\\) gives the symmetric cluster matrix\n\\[\n\\dot M_{ij}\n=\n-2\\lambda\\int_{S^2}u_iu_j\n\\left(\\dot w-\\int_{S^2}\\dot w\\,d\\mu_w\\right)d\\mu_w.\n\\tag{2.6}\n\\]\nTerms from changing the orthonormal frame cancel at a scalar cluster matrix. The mean term is scalar, so the two traceless entries on the pair are represented, up to fixed nonzero factors, by\n\\[\nU_1=u^2-v^2,\\qquad U_2=2uv.\n\\]\nBoth have mean zero.</p><p>These functions are linearly independent. Otherwise a nonzero traceless quadratic form in \\((u,v)\\) would vanish everywhere. Its zero set in \\(\\mathbb R^2\\) is the union of two lines. On a connected neighborhood where \\((u,v)\\neq(0,0)\\), continuity puts \\((u,v)\\) in one line after shrinking the neighborhood. A fixed nonzero linear combination of \\(u,v\\) then vanishes on an open set. Unique continuation forces it to vanish globally, contradicting orthonormality.</p><p>Thus the Gram matrix of \\(U_1,U_2\\) is positive definite, and testing conformal directions \\(U_1,U_2\\) proves rank two. If \\(u,v\\) have the same parity, both product functions are even.</p><p>Finally, conjugating by the parameter-dependent pullback adds a commutator with the Laplacian. Its compression to an eigenspace on which the operator is \\(\\lambda I\\) is zero. Hence the coordinate change does not alter the splitting matrix. The zero eigenvalue requires no splitting argument because \\(S^2\\) is connected.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Parameters whose metrics are simple through any fixed position are dense in \\(\\mathcal U\\).", "statement": "<p>**Finite-cutoff avoidance; original Lemma \\(2.2\\).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the parameter neighborhood \\(\\mathcal U\\) of Proposition \\(1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A metric is *simple through position \\(q\\)* if\n  \\[\n  \\lambda_1&lt;\\lambda_2&lt;\\cdots&lt;\\lambda_q&lt;\\lambda_{q+1}.\n  \\]\n  The final inequality is included.</p><p class=\"li\">In the antipodally even parameter family, use the same convention separately for the ordered even and odd lists, with prescribed finite cutoffs.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Parameters whose metrics are simple through any fixed position are dense in \\(\\mathcal U\\). Any two such parameters can be joined inside \\(\\mathcal U\\) by a smooth path simple through that position and constant near its endpoints.</p><p>In the even family, the analogous statements hold for simplicity through prescribed finite cutoffs within each parity. Cross-parity coincidences are allowed there.</p>", "proof": "<p>In the fixed-area gauge the operators have common domain \\(H^2(S^2)\\) in \\(L^2(\\mu)\\), and depend smoothly on finite parameters as maps \\(H^2\\to L^2\\). On a resolvent contour, elliptic estimates give bounded inverses \\(L^2\\to H^2\\). The inverse-difference identity and a Neumann series give smooth parameter dependence. Integrating the resolvent gives a smooth finite-rank spectral projection. Only smooth parameter dependence, not analytic eigenbranches, is needed.</p><p>Start with a smooth preliminary path \\(w_0(t)\\), \\(0\\leq t\\leq1\\), joining the two given parameters and constant near the endpoints. Convexity supplies such a path. Endpoint gaps persist on small initial and final intervals under sufficiently small perturbations. Choose a cutoff \\(\\chi(t)\\) vanishing near \\(0,1\\) and equal to \\(1\\) outside those simple intervals.</p><p>Fix the cutoff \\(q\\). Uniform mass-form comparison bounds \\(\\lambda_{q+1}\\) above near the path. Choose a larger \\(\\Lambda\\). Every repeated cluster affecting simplicity through \\(q\\), including an entire cluster straddling the cutoff, then has eigenvalue below \\(\\Lambda\\). Reverse comparison uniformly bounds the number of eigenvalues below \\(\\Lambda\\).</p><p>Consider all triples \\((t,u,v)\\) with\n\\[\n-\\Delta_{h^0(w_0(t))}u=\\lambda u,\\qquad\n-\\Delta_{h^0(w_0(t))}v=\\lambda v,\\qquad\n0&lt;\\lambda\\leq\\Lambda,\n\\]\nwhere \\(u,v\\) are real and orthonormal for \\(d\\mu_{w_0(t)}\\). This set is compact in the smooth topology. Indeed,\n\\[\n-\\Delta_0u=\\lambda\\rho_{w_0(t)}u\n\\]\nhas uniformly smooth coefficients, uniformly equivalent mass norms, and bounded \\(\\lambda\\). Elliptic estimates and Sobolev embedding give all spatial derivative bounds. A diagonal subsequence converges smoothly, preserving the equation and orthonormality. The first positive eigenvalue is uniformly bounded away from zero. In the parity version restrict to same-parity pairs, a closed condition.</p><p>At each such pair, Lemma \\(1.2\\) supplies two directions giving an invertible derivative of the two traceless entries. This condition is open. A finite cover supplies one finite pool \\(\\xi_1,\\ldots,\\xi_D\\), whose span \\(E\\) has rank two on every relevant pair. In the parity version the directions are even. The same rank holds at relevant eigenfunction pairs in a sufficiently small \\(E\\)-neighborhood of the path: otherwise elliptic compactness would produce a limiting counterexample on the path.</p><p>Use the finite family\n\\[\nw(t,z)=w_0(t)+\\chi(t)\\sum_{j=1}^Dz_j\\xi_j.\n\\]\nFor small \\(z\\), it stays in \\(\\mathcal U\\), and endpoint intervals remain simple. At every possible bad point outside those intervals, \\(\\chi=1\\).</p><p>At a bad point, let \\(r,\\ldots,s\\) be the entire repeated block, with strict gaps outside it. A contour enclosing precisely that eigenvalue gives a smooth rank-\\(m\\) projection, \\(m=s-r+1\\). Project and orthonormalize a basis to obtain a smooth real frame and a symmetric cluster matrix \\(M(t,z)\\). Choose the first two frame vectors at the center to be any orthonormal pair in the eigenspace, and put\n\\[\nF(t,z)=\\bigl(M_{11}(t,z)-M_{22}(t,z),\\,2M_{12}(t,z)\\bigr).\n\\]\nIts derivative in \\(z\\) has rank two at the center and nearby. Thus \\(F^{-1}(0)\\) is locally a codimension-two submanifold.</p><p>If the same block \\(r,\\ldots,s\\) remains entirely equal, then \\(M\\) is scalar and \\(F=0\\). This is the only containment asserted. Partial repetitions of a larger cluster have their own charts centered on their smaller exact repeated blocks.</p><p>For each pair \\(r,s\\), the stratum where that entire block is repeated, with strict exterior gaps, has a countable subcover by such neighborhoods, since it is a subspace of the second-countable space \\([0,1]\\times\\mathbb R^D\\). The union over all pairs covers all bad points by countably many codimension-two zero sets. Each projects to a null set in \\(\\mathbb R^D\\): in an implicit-function chart, two coordinates of \\(z\\) are smooth functions of \\(t\\) and the remaining \\(D-2\\) coordinates, so the projection is locally a Lipschitz image of dimension \\(D-1\\). Hence arbitrarily small \\(z\\) avoid every bad point. The endpoints are fixed.</p><p>For density, repeat the argument without time. The exact-block zero sets have codimension two in the finite parameter space, so their countable union is null and its complement dense. In the even family, use the common pool of even directions for the two parity lists. The union of their bad sets remains countable and null.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every neighborhood of \\(w_*\\) in \\(\\mathcal U\\) contains a parameter for which positions \\(i,i+1\\) remain equal and all other inequalities through position \\(q\\), including the gap after \\(q\\), are strict.", "statement": "<p>**Retaining an isolated double; original Lemma \\(2.3\\).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(h^0(w_*)\\), with \\(w_*\\in\\mathcal U\\), has an isolated eigenvalue of exact multiplicity two at positions \\(i,i+1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(q\\geq i+1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Unlike the parity-restricted setting of Lemma \\(1.3\\), perturbations here are unrestricted conformal perturbations, even if \\(w_*\\) was obtained in the even family.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every neighborhood of \\(w_*\\) in \\(\\mathcal U\\) contains a parameter for which positions \\(i,i+1\\) remain equal and all other inequalities through position \\(q\\), including the gap after \\(q\\), are strict.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(u,v\\) be an orthonormal basis for the chosen double at the current parameter. Put\n\\[\nU_1=u^2-v^2,\\qquad U_2=2uv.\n\\]\nThe linearized double-preserving constraints have kernel\n\\[\n\\mathcal K=\n\\left\\{\\xi\\in C^\\infty(S^2;\\mathbb R):\n\\int U_1\\xi\\,d\\mu_w=\\int U_2\\xi\\,d\\mu_w=0\\right\\}.\n\\]\nBy Lemma \\(1.2\\), this has codimension two.</p><p>We first show that every different repeated positive eigenspace admits a direction in \\(\\mathcal K\\) with nonscalar compression. Suppose otherwise for an eigenspace of eigenvalue \\(\\mu_*&gt;0\\), and choose an orthonormal pair \\(a,b\\) in it. The independent functionals represented by\n\\[\nA_1=a^2-b^2,\\qquad A_2=2ab\n\\]\nwould both vanish on \\(\\mathcal K\\). A functional vanishing on the kernel of the surjection\n\\[\n\\xi\\longmapsto\\left(\\int U_1\\xi\\,d\\mu_w,\\int U_2\\xi\\,d\\mu_w\\right)\n\\]\nis a linear combination of its components. Independence therefore implies equality of the two spans of functionals, and hence the pointwise identity\n\\[\nQ(a,b)=TQ(u,v),\\qquad Q(x,y)=(x^2-y^2,2xy),\n\\]\nfor an invertible constant real \\(2\\times2\\) matrix \\(T\\). There is no additive constant: all four product functions have mean zero.</p><p>This identity forces equality of nodal sets. Namely,\n\\[\na=0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\nQ(a,b)\\in\\{(-t,0):t\\geq0\\}.\n\\]\nThe inverse image of that closed ray under \\(T\\) is another closed ray. Under the identification \\(\\mathbb R^2=\\mathbb C\\), \\(Q\\) is squaring, whose inverse image of a closed ray is one unoriented line. Consequently, for some nonzero \\((c,d)\\),\n\\[\n\\{a=0\\}=\\{cu+dv=0\\}.\n\\]\nThe origin is included in the rays, so this also covers simultaneous zeros.</p><p>Write \\(f=cu+dv\\), a nonzero eigenfunction for the chosen eigenvalue \\(\\lambda_*\\). On a connected component \\(\\Omega\\) of the common nonzero set, change signs so that \\(a,f&gt;0\\). Both restrictions belong to \\(H_0^1(\\Omega)\\), with no regularity assumption on the nodal boundary. For example,\n\\[\nf_\\varepsilon=(f-\\varepsilon)_+\n\\]\nhas compact support in \\(\\Omega\\), and converges to \\(f\\) in \\(H^1(\\Omega)\\) by dominated convergence for functions and gradients. Each truncation is approximable by smooth compactly supported functions. The same holds for \\(a\\). Test the weak eigenvalue equations against one another to obtain\n\\[\n(\\mu_*-\\lambda_*)\\int_\\Omega af\\,d\\mu_w=0.\n\\]\nThe integral is positive, contradicting distinct eigenvalues. Thus a nonscalar direction \\(\\xi\\in\\mathcal K\\) exists.</p><p>Choose directions \\(\\zeta_1,\\zeta_2\\) on which the derivative of the chosen double’s traceless entries is invertible. In\n\\[\nw(t,x_1,x_2)=w+t\\xi+x_1\\zeta_1+x_2\\zeta_2,\n\\]\nform its smooth \\(2\\times2\\) Riesz matrix \\(M_d\\) and constraints\n\\[\nF_d=(M_{d,11}-M_{d,22},\\,2M_{d,12}).\n\\]\nThe implicit-function theorem gives \\(x_1(t),x_2(t)\\) with \\(F_d=0\\). Because \\(\\xi\\in\\mathcal K\\), their derivatives at zero vanish. The curve has tangent \\(\\xi\\), and the unwanted cluster has matrix\n\\[\n\\mu_*I+tD+o(t)\n\\]\nwith \\(D\\) nonscalar. A sufficiently small nonzero \\(t\\) splits it into at least two smaller groups while retaining the chosen double.</p><p>Extend the initial list past \\(q+1\\) to the last member \\(q'\\) of the cluster containing \\(\\lambda_{q+1}\\). Preserve the gap after \\(q'\\), the chosen double’s isolation, and all strict gaps already obtained. If current multiplicities are \\(m_1,\\ldots,m_r\\), then\n\\[\n\\sum_j\\binom{m_j}{2}\n\\]\nstrictly decreases whenever an unwanted cluster splits; the chosen double contributes \\(1\\). Thus finitely many steps remove every other repetition. The total perturbation may be arbitrarily small, and isolation keeps the double at \\(i,i+1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.5", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every eigenvalue of the meridional profile problem is simple.", "statement": "<p>**Sector simplicity and reflection parity; original Lemma \\(2.4\\).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\zeta(\\theta)&gt;0\\) be smooth as an axisymmetric function on \\(S^2\\), with\n  \\[\n  \\zeta(\\pi-\\theta)=\\zeta(\\theta).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Consider \\(\\zeta^2g_0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a nonnegative integer azimuthal number \\(m\\) and one azimuthal factor, the constant factor when \\(m=0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the form domain obtained by closing smooth sphere sector profiles in the energy-plus-mass norm.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every eigenvalue of the meridional profile problem is simple. Its lowest profile is strictly positive on \\((0,\\pi)\\) and even under equatorial reflection.</p><p>Along a smooth reflection-symmetric deformation, every fixed position in the \\(m=0\\) list retains its reflection parity. In particular, the position occupied at round by degree \\(l\\) retains parity \\((-1)^l\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The separated profile equation is\n\\[\n-(\\sin\\theta\\,v')'+\\frac{m^2}{\\sin\\theta}v\n=\\lambda\\zeta^2\\sin\\theta\\,v.\n\\]\nCompact spectral theory on the smooth compact sphere, restricted to this closed sector, supplies eigenfunctions and the variational minimum. At a pole, profiles have the form \\(t^ma(t^2)\\) for \\(m\\geq1\\), and are smooth even functions of polar distance \\(t\\) for \\(m=0\\).</p><p>For two profiles at the same eigenvalue,\n\\[\nW=\\sin\\theta(uv'-u'v)\n\\]\nis constant. Pole behavior gives \\(W\\to0\\) at a pole: for \\(m\\geq1\\), the profiles are \\(O(t^m)\\) and derivatives \\(O(t^{m-1})\\); for \\(m=0\\), profiles are bounded and derivatives \\(O(t)\\). Thus \\(W=0\\). At an interior point where \\(u\\neq0\\), a scalar multiple of \\(u\\) has the same value and derivative as \\(v\\), so ODE uniqueness gives equality.</p><p>Replacing a real minimizer by its absolute value preserves its form domain, energy, and mass. The nonnegative minimizer is smooth inside. An interior zero would be a minimum with zero derivative and force the profile to vanish by ODE uniqueness. Hence the ground profile is positive. Reflection preserves the equation and pole conditions; simplicity and positivity make the normalized ground profile reflection even. With its azimuthal factor its antipodal parity is \\((-1)^m\\).</p><p>For \\(m=0\\), the Wronskian argument proves simplicity at every position. Min–max gives continuous ordered eigenvalues along a smooth deformation, and a local simple projection gives a continuous unit eigenfunction. Reflection acts on its line by \\(+1\\) or \\(-1\\), continuously and hence constantly. At round, the sign is that of the corresponding Legendre polynomial, \\((-1)^l\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.6", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every sufficiently large integer \\(k\\) and every \\(i\\) with \\(B_L+1\\leq i<p\\), there is \\(w\\in\\mathcal U\\) for which the only failure of simplicity through position \\(p+1\\) is …", "statement": "<p>**Adjacent doubles; original Lemma \\(2.5\\).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the conformal neighborhood, spectral numbering, and fixed-area gauge of Proposition \\(1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(\\vartheta&gt;1\\), and for \\(k\\) set\n  \\[\n  M=\\lfloor\\vartheta k\\rfloor,\\qquad L=\\lfloor\\sqrt{k}\\rfloor,\\qquad\n  p=(M+1)^2.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a prescribed sufficiently small neighborhood \\(\\mathcal U\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every sufficiently large integer \\(k\\) and every \\(i\\) with \\(B_L+1\\leq i&lt;p\\), there is \\(w\\in\\mathcal U\\) for which the only failure of simplicity through position \\(p+1\\) is\n\\[\n\\lambda_i(h(w))=\\lambda_{i+1}(h(w)).\n\\]\nThis eigenvalue has exact multiplicity two. In particular,\n\\[\n\\lambda_p(h(w))&lt;\\lambda_{p+1}(h(w)).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>First suppose the two positions lie inside a round degree-\\(l\\) block. Multiplication defines\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Sym}^2\\mathcal Y_l\\longrightarrow\n\\bigoplus_{j=0}^l\\mathcal Y_{2j},\\qquad u\\odot v\\longmapsto uv.\n\\]\nThe target contains products by homogeneous harmonic decomposition. The map is an isomorphism, as follows.</p><p>Its image is rotation invariant, hence invariant under the rotation Casimir \\(\\Delta_0\\). It contains\n\\[\nF_l(z)=(1-z^2)^l\n=\\bigl(\\operatorname{Re}(x+iy)^l\\bigr)^2\n+\\bigl(\\operatorname{Im}(x+iy)^l\\bigr)^2.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\Delta_0=(1-z^2)\\partial_z^2-2z\\partial_z\\) on functions of \\(z\\),\n\\[\n\\bigl(\\Delta_0+2l(2l+1)\\bigr)F_l=4l^2F_{l-1}.\n\\]\nExpand\n\\[\nF_l=\\sum_{j=0}^lc_{lj}P_{2j}(z).\n\\]\nThe top coefficient is nonzero by polynomial degree. For \\(j&lt;l\\),\n\\[\nc_{lj}=\n\\frac{4l^2}{2l(2l+1)-2j(2j+1)}c_{l-1,j}.\n\\]\nStarting with \\(F_0=1\\), all coefficients are nonzero. Polynomial spectral projections in \\(\\Delta_0\\) therefore place a nonzero zonal vector of each \\(\\mathcal Y_{2j}\\) in the image. Rotations of a nonzero zonal harmonic span its harmonic space: they are harmonic projections of powers of linear forms, and those powers span homogeneous polynomials. Thus the map is onto. Source and target both have dimension\n\\[\n\\frac{(2l+1)(2l+2)}2\n=\\sum_{j=0}^l(4j+1)=(l+1)(2l+1).\n\\]</p><p>Dualizing this isomorphism in the first-variation formula of Lemma \\(1.2\\) shows that the full round block’s trace-free variation map is onto the trace-free symmetric matrices. Explicitly, a nonzero trace-free matrix \\(C\\) gives the nonzero product function \\(\\sum C_{\\alpha\\beta}u_\\alpha u_\\beta\\), so cannot annihilate all conformal directions. Choose finitely many directions making the derivative onto and represent the cluster by its smooth Riesz matrix. The submersion theorem realizes every sufficiently small trace-free matrix as its trace-free part. Choose a diagonal matrix with strictly increasing ordered entries except for the designated adjacent equality. Round exterior gaps remain positive. Lemma \\(1.4\\), with cutoff \\(p+1\\), removes every other repetition while retaining this isolated double.</p><p>Now suppose the pair crosses consecutive round blocks, so \\(i=B_l\\), \\(L+1\\leq l\\leq M-1\\). Set \\(P=(-1)^l\\) and \\(c_0=3/2\\). Choose a fixed smooth axisymmetric equatorial bump \\(\\psi\\), supported away from the poles, with\n\\[\n\\psi(\\pi-\\theta)=\\psi(\\theta),\\quad\n0\\leq\\psi\\leq1,\\quad \\psi(\\pi/2)=1,\\quad\n\\bar\\psi=\\frac1\\pi\\int_0^\\pi\\psi(\\theta)\\,d\\theta&lt;\\frac1{10}.\n\\]\nFor large \\(l\\), consider\n\\[\n\\widehat h_l=\\zeta_l^2g_0,\\qquad\n\\zeta_l=1-\\frac{c_0}{l}\\psi.\n\\]\nIt is antipodally invariant and tends to \\(g_0\\) in every fixed smooth norm. Its parameter is \\(w_l=\\log\\zeta_l\\). Area normalization multiplies all eigenvalues by one positive number and does not alter ordering.</p><p>The unchanged Dirichlet form and\n\\[\n(1-c_0/l)^2\\leq\\zeta_l^2\\leq1\n\\]\nplace the positions belonging at round to degree \\(q\\), in either parity list, in\n\\[\n[q(q+1),(1-c_0/l)^{-2}q(q+1)].\n\\tag{2.17}\n\\]\nThe intervals for \\(q,q+2\\) in the same parity are disjoint through \\(q=l+1\\), for large \\(l\\), because\n\\[\n\\bigl((1-c_0/l)^{-2}-1\\bigr)q(q+1)\n\\leq(2c_0+o(1))(q+1)&lt;4q+6.\n\\]\nThus these degree groups retain their order within each parity list.</p><p>Take the lowest eigenvalue in azimuthal sector \\(m=l\\). By Lemma \\(1.5\\), its ground profile is positive, unique up to scale, and reflection even, so the resulting mode has parity \\(P\\). Choose a real eigenfunction \\(u_l\\) with \\(\\int u_l^2\\,d\\mu=1\\), and write\n\\[\nE_l=\\int|\\nabla_0u_l|^2\\,d\\mu,\\qquad\nM_l=\\int\\zeta_l^2u_l^2\\,d\\mu.\n\\]\nThe azimuthal term gives\n\\[\nE_l\\geq l^2\\int\\frac{u_l^2}{\\sin^2\\theta}\\,d\\mu,\\qquad M_l\\leq1.\n\\]\nSector min–max gives\n\\[\n\\frac{E_l}{M_l}\\leq(1-c_0/l)^{-2}l(l+1)=l^2+O(l).\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\int(\\sin^{-2}\\theta-1)u_l^2\\,d\\mu=O(l^{-1}),\n\\]\nso the probability masses \\(u_l^2d\\mu\\) concentrate at the equator. Hence\n\\[\n\\int\\psi u_l^2\\,d\\mu=1-o(1),\\qquad\nM_l=1-\\frac{2c_0}{l}+o(l^{-1}).\n\\]\nThe lowest round eigenvalue in this sector is \\(l(l+1)\\), so\n\\[\n\\lambda_{\\mathrm{high}}\n=\\frac{E_l}{M_l}\n\\geq l^2+(1+2c_0)l+o(l).\n\\tag{2.18}\n\\]\nIts bounds locate it in the parity-\\(P\\) degree-\\(l\\) group.</p><p>For the low value, use the axisymmetric eigenfunction in the slot occupied at round by degree \\(l+1\\), namely slot \\(l+2\\). Lemma \\(1.5\\) makes that value simple throughout the reflection-symmetric deformation and gives parity \\(-P\\).</p><p>Set\n\\[\nR=\\frac1\\pi\\int_0^\\pi\\zeta_l(\\theta)\\,d\\theta\n=1-\\frac{c_0\\bar\\psi}{l},\\qquad\nx(\\theta)=\\frac1R\\int_0^\\theta\\zeta_l(t)\\,dt.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\widehat h_l=R^2\\bigl(dx^2+\\sin^2x\\,F(x)^2d\\phi^2\\bigr),\\qquad\nF(x)=\\frac{\\zeta_l(\\theta(x))\\sin\\theta(x)}{R\\sin x}.\n\\]\nThe positive reflection-symmetric \\(F\\) has smooth even extensions at both endpoints and\n\\[\nF=1+O(l^{-1})\n\\]\nin every fixed smooth norm. Near a pole, \\(\\zeta_l=1\\), so \\(\\theta=Rx\\) and\n\\[\nF=\\frac{\\sin(Rx)}{R\\sin x},\n\\]\nwhich gives the claimed extension and bounds.</p><p>The axisymmetric nonnegative Laplacian of the parenthesized metric is\n\\[\n-\\frac1{\\sin x\\,F}\\partial_x(\\sin x\\,F\\,\\partial_x)\n\\]\non \\(L^2(\\sin x\\,F\\,dx)\\). Multiplication by \\(\\sqrt F\\) maps the smooth even pole-regular core bijectively to the round core. The boundary contribution\n\\[\n\\left[\\sin x\\,(\\sqrt F)'(\\sqrt F)^{-1}|v|^2\\right]_0^\\pi\n\\]\nvanishes. The conjugated form is the round axisymmetric form plus\n\\[\nV_F=\\frac{(\\sqrt F)''+\\cot x\\,(\\sqrt F)'}{\\sqrt F},\n\\qquad \\|V_F\\|_\\infty=O(l^{-1}).\n\\]\nEven endpoint extensions remove the apparent singularities. This bounded potential makes shifted form norms equivalent, so the multiplication also maps the closed form domains onto one another. Min–max gives\n\\[\n\\lambda_{\\mathrm{low}}\n=R^{-2}\\bigl((l+1)(l+2)+O(l^{-1})\\bigr)\n=l^2+(3+2c_0\\bar\\psi)l+O(1).\n\\tag{2.19}\n\\]\nForm comparison and the separated parity intervals locate this slot in the parity-\\(-P\\) degree-\\((l+1)\\) group. Therefore\n\\[\n\\lambda_{\\mathrm{high}}-\\lambda_{\\mathrm{low}}\n\\geq\\bigl(2c_0(1-\\bar\\psi)-2\\bigr)l+o(l)\n=(1-3\\bar\\psi)l+o(l)&gt;0.\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(\\lambda_j^P,\\lambda_j^{-P}\\) be the ordered parity eigenvalues. The counts through round degree \\(l\\) are\n\\[\nA_l=\\frac{(l+1)(l+2)}2,\\qquad\nD_l=\\frac{l(l+1)}2,\\qquad A_l+D_l=B_l.\n\\]\nAt round,\n\\[\n\\lambda_{A_l}^P=l(l+1)&lt;(l+1)(l+2)=\\lambda_{D_l+1}^{-P},\n\\]\nwhile at \\(\\widehat h_l\\),\n\\[\n\\lambda_{A_l}^P\\geq\\lambda_{\\mathrm{high}}\n&gt;\\lambda_{\\mathrm{low}}\\geq\\lambda_{D_l+1}^{-P}.\n\\]\nThese strict indexed inequalities survive normalization and small endpoint perturbations. By Lemma \\(1.3\\), choose endpoints simple through the needed parity cutoffs, and join them by an even path with that property. The continuous difference\n\\[\n\\lambda_{A_l}^P-\\lambda_{D_l+1}^{-P}\n\\]\nchanges sign. At a zero the two values are simple in their parity lists, and exactly\n\\[\n(A_l-1)+D_l=B_l-1\n\\]\ncombined eigenvalues lie below them. Hence the equality is an exact double at \\(B_l,B_l+1\\). Apply Lemma \\(1.4\\) in unrestricted directions to retain it and separate every other level through \\(p+1\\).</p><p>The cross-block estimates hold uniformly for \\(l\\geq L+1\\) once \\(L\\) is large. The parameters \\(w_l\\) tend smoothly to zero uniformly there; all remaining perturbations may be arbitrarily small. Thus one prescribed \\(\\mathcal U\\) contains all selected metrics for every sufficiently large \\(k\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are \\(C>0\\) and, after \\(C\\) is fixed, an integer \\(J_0\\), such that every integer \\(J\\geq J_0\\) admits a smooth \\(f=f_J:[0,\\infty)\\to[0,\\infty)\\), independent of \\(z\\), for which \\( g_z=dr^2+f(r)^2H_z(\\log r) \\) …", "statement": "<p>**Uniform radial realization; original Proposition \\(4.1\\), with the comparison from Section \\(4.1\\).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix one finite angular program \\(w(s)\\), \\(H(s)=h(w(s))\\), from Proposition \\(1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Extend \\(w\\) on \\(-2&lt;s&lt;0\\) to the round value \\(0\\), staying in the same small neighborhood, and set \\(w(s)=0\\) for \\(s\\leq-2\\). The reference path is constant near phase \\(0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(0&lt;a&lt;1\\) and \\(a^2&lt;\\kappa_*&lt;1\\). Require the reference program, including the negative-phase interpolation, to have Gauss curvature uniformly greater than \\(\\kappa_*\\).</p><p class=\"li\">At successive crossings choose smooth directions \\(\\psi_\\nu\\) and smooth phase bumps \\(\\chi_\\nu\\). Their supports are disjoint, have bounded phase width, lie where the selected variation coefficient has one nonzero sign, and equal \\(1\\) on smaller crossing neighborhoods. There are finitely many smooth types.</p><p class=\"li\">For a large integer \\(J\\), put\n  \\[\n  \\eta(t)=t^{-3/4},\\qquad\n  s(t)=4(t^{1/4}-J^{1/4}),\\qquad s'(t)=\\eta(t).\n  \\]\n  If \\(s(t_\\nu)=s_\\nu\\) is crossing \\(\\nu\\), define, for \\(z=(z_\\nu)\\in[-1,1]^{\\mathbb N}\\),\n  \\[\n  w_z(t)=w(s(t))+\n  \\sum_\\nu z_\\nu\\eta(t_\\nu)^{3/4}\\chi_\\nu(s(t))\\psi_\\nu,\n  \\qquad H_z(t)=h(w_z(t)).\n  \\]\n  For \\(t\\leq0\\), set \\(H_z(t)=g_0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are \\(C&gt;0\\) and, after \\(C\\) is fixed, an integer \\(J_0\\), such that every integer \\(J\\geq J_0\\) admits a smooth \\(f=f_J:[0,\\infty)\\to[0,\\infty)\\), independent of \\(z\\), for which\n\\[\ng_z=dr^2+f(r)^2H_z(\\log r)\n\\]\nextends to a complete smooth metric on \\(\\mathbb R^3\\), Euclidean near zero, with\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ric}_{g_z}\\geq0,\n\\]\nstrictly positive Ricci curvature outside a compact set, and\n\\[\nd_{g_z}(0,(r,x))=r.\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\na\\leq f'(r)\\leq1,\\qquad f'(r)\\to a,\\qquad\nb(t):=e^{-t}f(e^t)\\to a.\n\\]\nFor \\(t\\geq0.8J\\), uniformly over all controls, including after every fixed number of angular differentiations,\n\\[\n\\partial_tH_z=O(\\eta),\\qquad\n\\partial_t^2H_z=O(\\eta^2),\\qquad\n\\operatorname{tr}(H_z^{-1}\\partial_tH_z)=0,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nb_{tt}+b_t=-C\\eta^2,\\qquad\nb_t=O(C\\eta^2),\\qquad b_{tt}=O(C\\eta^2),\\qquad\nb-a=O(Ct^{-1/2}).\n\\]\nIn every fixed smooth angular norm,\n\\[\nH_z(t)-H(s(t))=O(\\eta(t)^{3/4}).\n\\]\nThe controlled links lie in a fixed compact subset of a finite-dimensional smooth family and have \\(K_{H_z}&gt;\\kappa_*\\). Constants may depend on the fixed angular program, \\(a,\\kappa_*,C\\), but not on \\(J\\geq J_0\\) or \\(z\\).</p><p>If a prescribed \\(0&lt;\\delta&lt;1\\) is imposed through the angular neighborhood, with the reference program and interpolation chosen with positive margin, then after increasing \\(J\\),\n\\[\na^2(1-\\delta)g_E\\leq g_z\\leq(1+\\delta)g_E\n\\qquad\\text{on }\\mathbb R^3.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Lemmas \\(2.1\\) and \\(2.2\\), choosing the curvature buffer \\(C\\) below before choosing \\(J\\).</p><p>The first angular motion occurs after \\(s(t)=-2\\), at\n\\[\nt_{-2}=(J^{1/4}-1/2)^4\n=J-2J^{3/4}+O(J^{1/2})&gt;0.8J\n\\]\nfor large \\(J\\). Thus the exact radial tail applies throughout angular motion. Before that, the metric is round-warped, with\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ric}_{rr}=-2f''/f\\geq0,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{Ric}^T=\n\\left(\\frac{1-(f')^2}{f^2}-\\frac{f''}{f}\\right)I\\geq0.\n\\]</p><p>During angular motion put\n\\[\nA=H_z^{-1}\\partial_tH_z,\\quad B=\\tfrac12A,\\quad\nq=1+b_t/b,\\quad t=\\log r.\n\\]\nThe shape operator is\n\\[\nS=\\frac1r(qI+B),\\qquad \\operatorname{tr}S=\\frac{2q}{r}.\n\\]\nThe normal, Codazzi, and Gauss identities give\n\\[\nr^2\\operatorname{Ric}_{rr}\n=-\\frac{2(b_{tt}+b_t)}b-\\frac14\\operatorname{tr}(A^2),\n\\tag{4.9}\n\\]\n\\[\nr^2\\operatorname{Ric}(\\partial_r,X/f)\n=b^{-1}(\\operatorname{div}_{H_z}B)(X),\n\\tag{4.10}\n\\]\nfor an \\(H_z\\)-unit vector \\(X\\), and\n\\[\nr^2\\operatorname{Ric}^T\n=\n\\left(\\frac{K_{H_z}}{b^2}-2q^2+q-q_t\\right)I\n+(1-2q)B-B_t.\n\\tag{4.11}\n\\]\nThe first follows also from \\(-\\partial_r\\operatorname{tr}S-\\operatorname{tr}(S^2)\\); the last uses\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ric}^T=f^{-2}K_{H_z}I-\\partial_rS-(\\operatorname{tr}S)S.\n\\]</p><p>The angular bounds give constants \\(M_A,M_M\\), independent of \\(C\\) and controls, with\n\\[\n|A|\\leq M_A\\eta,\\qquad\n|b^{-1}\\operatorname{div}_{H_z}B|\\leq M_M\\eta.\n\\]\nSince \\(q=1+O(C\\eta^2)\\), \\(q_t=O(C\\eta^2)\\),\n\\[\nr^2\\operatorname{Ric}^T\n=\\left(\\frac{K_{H_z}}{b^2}-1\\right)I+O(\\eta+C\\eta^2).\n\\]\nThe slack\n\\[\n\\tau_0=\\kappa_*/a^2-1&gt;0\n\\]\npermits \\(\\tau=\\tau_0/4\\), and then, for sufficiently late \\(J\\) after \\(C\\) is fixed,\n\\[\nr^2\\operatorname{Ric}^T\\geq\\tau I.\n\\]\nAlso,\n\\[\nr^2\\operatorname{Ric}_{rr}\n\\geq\\left(\\frac{2C}{b}-\\frac{M_A^2}{4}\\right)\\eta^2,\n\\qquad\n|r^2\\operatorname{Ric}_{\\mathrm{mixed}}|\\leq M_M\\eta.\n\\]\nChoose\n\\[\n2C&gt;\\frac{M_A^2}{4}+\\frac{M_M^2}{\\tau}.\n\\]\nBecause \\(b\\leq1\\), the Schur complement is bounded below by\n\\[\n\\left(2C-\\frac{M_A^2}{4}-\\frac{M_M^2}{\\tau}\\right)\\eta^2&gt;0.\n\\]\nThus Ricci curvature is positive throughout angular motion, uniformly in all controls. Before motion, the round-warped formulas give positive Ricci wherever the exact tail has begun, since \\(f''&lt;0\\). In particular it is positive for \\(r\\geq e^{0.7J}\\), and nonnegative everywhere.</p><p>Near zero, \\(f=r\\) and \\(H_z=g_0\\), so the metric extends as Euclidean. Every path from the origin to \\((r,x)\\) has length at least \\(r\\), while the radial segment has length \\(r\\). Hence the distance is exactly \\(r\\). Closed balls are compact in smooth polar coordinates, proving completeness.</p><p>For tensor comparison, choose the angular neighborhood using Lemma \\(1.1\\), with the reference program and interpolation in a smaller neighborhood with positive margin. Increasing \\(J\\) keeps all controls inside it, so\n\\[\n(1-\\delta)g_0\\leq H_z(t)\\leq(1+\\delta)g_0.\n\\]\nSince \\(a\\leq f(r)/r\\leq1\\),\n\\[\na^2(1-\\delta)g_E\\leq g_z\\leq(1+\\delta)g_E.\n\\]\nThese bounds extend across the Euclidean origin. Integrating along curves gives the corresponding distance bounds.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For sufficiently large \\(J\\), every control sequence defines a smooth angular path with fixed round area form, and \\( \\partial_tw_z=O(\\eta),\\qquad \\partial_t^2w_z=O(\\eta^2). \\) The corresponding \\(H_z\\) satisfies all …", "statement": "<p>**Uniform controlled angular bounds.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the controlled paths and fixed finite angular data of Proposition \\(2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For sufficiently large \\(J\\), every control sequence defines a smooth angular path with fixed round area form, and\n\\[\n\\partial_tw_z=O(\\eta),\\qquad \\partial_t^2w_z=O(\\eta^2).\n\\]\nThe corresponding \\(H_z\\) satisfies all angular estimates and compact-family assertions in Proposition \\(2\\). If\n\\[\nA=H_z^{-1}\\partial_tH_z,\\qquad B=\\tfrac12A,\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\nA=O(\\eta),\\qquad A_t=O(\\eta^2),\\qquad\n\\operatorname{div}_{H_z}A=O(\\eta),\n\\]\nand \\(A,B\\) are traceless.</p>", "proof": "<p>On a fixed-width phase bump,\n\\[\n|t-t_\\nu|=O(\\eta(t_\\nu)^{-1})=O(t_\\nu^{3/4}),\n\\]\nso \\(\\eta(t)/\\eta(t_\\nu)=1+o(1)\\) uniformly as \\(t_\\nu\\to\\infty\\). Since\n\\[\n\\eta'(t)=-\\tfrac34t^{-7/4}=O(\\eta(t)^2),\n\\]\ndifferentiating the controlled parameter formula, using bounded phase derivatives, gives\n\\[\n\\partial_tw_z=O(\\eta),\\qquad \\partial_t^2w_z=O(\\eta^2).\n\\]\nThe same holds after fixed angular differentiations and through the negative-phase interpolation, where controls vanish.</p><p>At most one perturbation is active at a time, and supports have no finite accumulation. Thus every control sequence gives a smooth path. Smoothness of the gauge on the fixed compact family gives the estimates for \\(H_z\\), including\n\\[\nH_z-H(s(t))=O(\\eta^{3/4}),\n\\]\nand the asserted estimates for \\(A,A_t,\\operatorname{div}A\\).</p><p>Differentiating the fixed area form gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}(H_z^{-1}\\partial_tH_z)=0.\n\\]\nControl amplitudes tend uniformly to zero as \\(J\\to\\infty\\). Compactness of the reference program, including interpolation, therefore gives \\(K_{H_z}&gt;\\kappa_*\\) for all controls once \\(J\\) is large.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For sufficiently large \\(J\\), there is a smooth concave \\(f\\), Euclidean near zero, with \\( a\\leq f'\\leq1,\\qquad f'\\to a, \\) whose associated \\(b(t)=e^{-t}f(e^t)\\) satisfies all radial estimates of Proposition \\(2\\) on …", "statement": "<p>**Construction and estimates for the common radial factor.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(a\\in(0,1)\\) and a constant \\(C&gt;0\\), to be chosen for curvature in Proposition \\(2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(0\\leq\\theta\\leq1\\) be smooth, zero on \\((-\\infty,1/2]\\), and one on \\([0.7,\\infty)\\), and set \\(\\theta_J(t)=\\theta(t/J)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\beta\\geq0\\) be smooth, supported in \\((1,2)\\), with \\(\\int\\beta(r)\\,dr=1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For sufficiently large \\(J\\), there is a smooth concave \\(f\\), Euclidean near zero, with\n\\[\na\\leq f'\\leq1,\\qquad f'\\to a,\n\\]\nwhose associated \\(b(t)=e^{-t}f(e^t)\\) satisfies all radial estimates of Proposition \\(2\\) on \\(t\\geq0.8J\\), uniformly for every such \\(J\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Define\n\\[\nf_{\\mathrm{tail}}''(r)=\n\\begin{cases}\n-\\dfrac{C}{r}\\theta_J(\\log r)(\\log r)^{-3/2},\n&amp;r&gt;e^{J/2},\\\\{}[4pt]\n0,&amp;0\\leq r\\leq e^{J/2}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nIts total slope loss is at most \\(2C(J/2)^{-1/2}\\). After fixing \\(C\\), take \\(J\\) large enough that it is less than \\(1-a\\), and put\n\\[\nm_J=1-a-C\\int_{J/2}^{\\infty}\\theta_J(t)t^{-3/2}\\,dt&gt;0.\n\\]\nSet\n\\[\nf''=-m_J\\beta+f_{\\mathrm{tail}}'',\\qquad f(0)=0,\\quad f'(0)=1.\n\\]\nThen \\(f=r\\) near zero, \\(f''\\leq0\\), and the total slope loss is exactly \\(1-a\\). Thus \\(a\\leq f'\\leq1\\), \\(f&gt;0\\) on \\((0,\\infty)\\), and \\(f'\\to a\\).</p><p>The function \\(b\\) is the average of \\(f'\\) on \\([0,e^t]\\), so \\(a\\leq b\\leq1\\) and \\(b\\to a\\). For \\(t\\geq0.7J\\), the tail is exact and\n\\[\nb_t+b=f'(e^t),\\qquad b_{tt}+b_t=-C\\eta(t)^2,\n\\qquad f'(e^t)=a+2Ct^{-1/2}.\n\\]\nWrite \\(u=0.7J\\). Concavity gives \\(|b_t(u)|\\leq1-a\\), and\n\\[\nb_t(t)=e^{-(t-u)}b_t(u)\n-C\\int_u^te^{-(t-v)}v^{-3/2}\\,dv.\n\\]\nFor \\(u\\geq3\\), the inequality \\(\\log(t/v)\\leq(t-v)/u\\) bounds the integral by \\(2t^{-3/2}\\). For \\(J\\geq15\\), \\(t\\geq0.8J\\),\n\\[\nt^{3/2}e^{-(t-u)}\n\\leq(0.8J)^{3/2}e^{-0.1J}\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nOne threshold therefore gives, for all later starts,\n\\[\n|b_t|\\leq3C\\eta^2,\\qquad |b_{tt}|\\leq4C\\eta^2,\\qquad\n0\\leq b-a\\leq5Ct^{-1/2}.\n\\]\nHere \\(C\\) is fixed before \\(J\\) tends to infinity.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every allowed start and every control sequence, \\( \\operatorname{AVR}(g_z)=a^2. \\) If \\(t_n\\to\\infty\\) and \\(s(t_n)\\bmod P\\to s_*\\), then, with \\(r_n=e^{t_n}\\) …", "statement": "<p>**Volume, cone limits, and negative radial sectional curvature; original Section \\(4.2\\).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use any metric \\(g_z\\) from Proposition \\(2\\), constructed from an angular program of Proposition \\(1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(P\\) be the period of the reference metric path \\(H(s)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every allowed start and every control sequence,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{AVR}(g_z)=a^2.\n\\]\nIf \\(t_n\\to\\infty\\) and \\(s(t_n)\\bmod P\\to s_*\\), then, with \\(r_n=e^{t_n}\\),\n\\[\n(\\mathbb R^3,r_n^{-2}g_z,0)\n\\longrightarrow\n\\bigl(C(S^2,a^2H(s_*)),0\\bigr)\n\\]\nin pointed Gromov–Hausdorff topology, smoothly away from the tip. Every phase occurs.</p><p>There are uncountably many pairwise nonisometric pointed tangent cones at infinity. There are also points tending to infinity with negative sectional curvature on a plane containing the radial direction.</p>", "proof": "<p>The fixed angular area form and exact distance identity give\n\\[\n\\operatorname{vol}_{g_z}B(0,R)\n=4\\pi\\int_0^Rf(r)^2\\,dr\n\\sim\\frac{4\\pi a^2}{3}R^3,\n\\]\nso \\(\\operatorname{AVR}(g_z)=a^2\\), independently of controls.</p><p>On a fixed rescaled annulus \\(0&lt;\\varepsilon\\leq\\rho=r/r_n\\leq R\\),\n\\[\ns(t_n+\\log\\rho)-s(t_n)\\to0,\\qquad\nb(t_n+\\log\\rho)\\to a,\n\\]\nuniformly, and all control perturbations vanish. The chain-rule estimates apply to each fixed derivative, yielding smooth convergence to\n\\[\nd\\rho^2+a^2\\rho^2H(s_*).\n\\]\nThe rescaled ball of radial size \\(\\varepsilon\\) has diameter at most \\(2\\varepsilon\\), so convergence extends across the cone tip in pointed Gromov–Hausdorff topology. Every phase occurs because \\(s(t)\\) is continuous, increasing, and unbounded.</p><p>The reference path has a phase simple through the chosen band and another with an isolated double in that band. Their ordered spectra differ. Thus some continuous ordered eigenvalue \\(\\lambda_j(H(s))\\) is nonconstant, and its image contains a nontrivial interval. Distinct values give distinct spectra of \\(a^2H(s)\\), hence nonisometric links. A pointed cone isometry preserves distance to the tip and restricts on the unit sphere to an intrinsic link isometry. Therefore these cones contain uncountably many isometry classes.</p><p>Choose a phase \\(s_*\\) with \\(H_s(s_*)\\not\\equiv0\\), which exists because the angular spectrum changes. At a point \\(x\\) where\n\\[\nD=H(s_*)^{-1}H_s(s_*)\n\\]\nis nonzero, \\(D\\) is self-adjoint and traceless, hence has a positive eigenvalue. Choose \\(t_n\\to\\infty\\) with \\(s(t_n)=s_*+nP\\). For the radial sectional-curvature endomorphism \\(K_r=-\\partial_rS-S^2\\),\n\\[\nr_n^2K_r\n=\\frac{C\\eta(t_n)^2}{b(t_n)}I\n+(1-2q)B-B_t-B^2\n=-\\frac{\\eta(t_n)}2D+o(\\eta(t_n)).\n\\]\nThe control contribution to \\(B\\) is \\(O(\\eta(t_n)^{7/4})\\), including differentiation of the bumps and smooth gauge. Thus a radial plane through a positive eigendirection of \\(D\\) has negative sectional curvature for all sufficiently late \\(n\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every \\(\\mathcal R^z_{r,R}\\) preserves positivity, constants, and the \\(\\mu\\)-mean, and is an \\(L^2(\\mu)\\)-contraction.", "statement": "<p>**Whole-ball restriction and frozen comparisons; original Lemma \\(5.1\\).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(k\\), its finite angular program, and the radial family from Propositions \\(1\\) and \\(2\\). All constants may depend on this fixed program and \\(p\\), but not on controls or sufficiently late starts.</p><p class=\"li\">Write\n  \\[\n  L(s)=-\\Delta_{H(s)},\\qquad \\delta_j=\\eta(j)^{3/4}=j^{-9/16}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(0&lt;r&lt;R\\), let \\(\\mathcal R^z_{r,R}\\) send boundary data on the radius-\\(R\\) sphere to the radius-\\(r\\) trace of its harmonic extension to the whole ball \\(B_{g_z}(0,R)\\). Identify both boundary spaces with \\(L^2(S^2,\\mu)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For integers \\(j\\geq J\\), put\n  \\[\n  T_j=\\mathcal R^z_{e^j,e^{j+1}},\n  \\]\n  and define\n  \\[\n  P_j^0=\\exp[-d(b(j)^{-2}L(s(j)))],\n  \\qquad\n  P_j^*=\\exp[-d(b(j)^{-2}(-\\Delta_{H_z(j)}))].\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every \\(\\mathcal R^z_{r,R}\\) preserves positivity, constants, and the \\(\\mu\\)-mean, and is an \\(L^2(\\mu)\\)-contraction. It smooths on every strictly inner sphere.</p><p>Uniformly over controls and sufficiently late starts,\n\\[\n\\|T_j-P_j^0\\|_{L^2\\to L^2}=o(1)\n\\qquad(j\\to\\infty).\n\\]\nFor the finite smooth reference frame,\n\\[\n\\|(T_j-P_j^*)e_i(s(j+1))\\|_2\n\\leq C\\left(\\eta(j)\\log\\frac1{\\eta(j)}+\\eta(j)^2\\right)\n=o(\\delta_j),\\qquad i\\leq p.\n\\]\nThe constants and remainders do not depend on controls at earlier radii. The sharper \\(o(\\delta_j)\\) assertion is only on the displayed finite frame, not in full operator norm.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma \\(4.1\\) proves all exact restriction and smoothing assertions. Lemma \\(4.2\\) proves the full operator-norm freezing estimate, and Lemma \\(4.3\\) proves the sharper finite-frame estimate with the asserted uniformity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The maps \\(\\mathcal R^z_{r,R}\\) have the positivity, constant, mean, contraction, and interior smoothing properties asserted in Proposition \\(4\\).", "statement": "<p>**Exact restriction properties and the logarithmic equation.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the metric family and exact restriction maps of Proposition \\(4\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The maps \\(\\mathcal R^z_{r,R}\\) have the positivity, constant, mean, contraction, and interior smoothing properties asserted in Proposition \\(4\\).</p><p>In logarithmic radius \\(t=\\log r\\), a harmonic function satisfies\n\\[\nu_{tt}+\\gamma(t)u_t+b(t)^{-2}\\Delta_{H_z(t)}u=0,\n\\qquad\n\\gamma(t)=1+\\frac{2b_t(t)}{b(t)},\n\\]\nwith divergence form\n\\[\n\\partial_t(e^tb(t)^2u_t)+e^t\\Delta_{H_z(t)}u=0.\n\\]\nOn fixed-width cylinders in the controlled end, local elliptic estimates have constants independent of large center time and controls.</p>", "proof": "<p>For smooth boundary data, the classical Dirichlet theorem gives a smooth harmonic extension inside the ball. Since the angular area form is fixed, away from the origin,\n\\[\nu_{rr}+2\\frac{f'}f u_r+f^{-2}\\Delta_{H_z(\\log r)}u=0.\n\\]\nFor \\(\\bar u(r)=\\int u(r,\\omega)\\,d\\mu(\\omega)\\),\n\\[\n(f(r)^2\\bar u_r(r))'=0.\n\\]\nRegularity in the Euclidean core, where \\(f=r\\), forces the integration constant to vanish. Thus every inner mean equals the boundary mean.</p><p>The maximum principle gives positivity and preservation of constants. A positive constant-preserving map \\(R\\) satisfies\n\\[\n(Rv)^2\\leq R(v^2):\n\\]\nat a fixed point apply positivity to \\(R((v-c)^2)\\), with \\(c=Rv\\) at that point. Integrating and using mean preservation gives\n\\[\n\\|\\mathcal R^z_{r,R}v\\|_2^2\n\\leq\\int\\mathcal R^z_{r,R}(v^2)\\,d\\mu\n=\\int v^2\\,d\\mu.\n\\]\nDensity extends the maps to \\(L^2\\).</p><p>These extensions remain traces of a harmonic function in the interior. Approximate boundary data in \\(L^2\\) by smooth data and use the contraction on inner spheres. Local \\(L^2\\)-to-sup estimates followed by interior Schauder estimates and their differentiated iterates make the extensions Cauchy in every smooth norm on smaller compact sets. The limit is harmonic; uniqueness follows from the same approximation. In particular, strictly inner traces are smooth.</p><p>Changing to \\(t=\\log r\\) gives the stated equation and divergence form. In a fixed angular atlas, the angular term is divergence with respect to the fixed density \\(\\mu\\). On a fixed-width time interval, division by \\(e^{t_0}\\) at its center leaves uniformly elliptic coefficients with uniform smooth bounds. The usual local estimates therefore have constants independent of large center time and controls. The compact core is covered by ordinary smooth charts.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Uniformly over controls and all sufficiently late starts, \\( \\|T_j-P_j^0\\|_{L^2\\to L^2}=o(1) \\qquad(j\\to\\infty). \\)", "statement": "<p>**Operator-norm freezing.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use \\(T_j,P_j^0\\) and the uniform end estimates of Proposition \\(4\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Uniformly over controls and all sufficiently late starts,\n\\[\n\\|T_j-P_j^0\\|_{L^2\\to L^2}=o(1)\n\\qquad(j\\to\\infty).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Take arbitrary \\(j_n\\to\\infty\\), allowed starts \\(J_n\\leq j_n\\), controls, and data \\(\\|v_n\\|_2\\leq1\\). Pass to a subsequence with\n\\[\nv_n\\rightharpoonup v,\\qquad H(s(j_n))\\to H_\\infty\n\\]\nsmoothly. Move the outer boundary to zero by \\(x=t-(j_n+1)\\), and let \\(u_n(x)\\) be the whole-ball extension.</p><p>On every fixed compact interval in \\(x\\leq0\\), coefficients converge smoothly to those of\n\\[\nU_{xx}+U_x+a^{-2}\\Delta_{H_\\infty}U=0.\n\\tag{5.9}\n\\]\nIndeed, phase changes are \\(O(\\eta(j_n))\\), controls vanish, and \\(b\\to a\\). The controlled interval extends an unbounded distance leftward because\n\\[\nj_n-0.8J_n\\geq0.2j_n.\n\\]</p><p>Lemma \\(4.1\\) gives \\(\\|u_n(x)\\|_2\\leq1\\) for every \\(x&lt;0\\). Interior \\(L^2\\)-to-sup and Schauder estimates give uniform \\(C^{2,\\alpha}\\)-bounds on smaller cylinders. Compact embedding and a diagonal subsequence give strong \\(L^2\\) convergence on each interior sphere, especially \\(x=-1\\). The limit solves (5.9) on \\((-\\infty,0)\\) and is sectionally bounded by \\(1\\).</p><p>To identify its boundary trace, fix smooth \\(\\varphi\\), and put\n\\[\ny_n(x)=\\langle u_n(x),\\varphi\\rangle_2.\n\\]\nSelf-adjointness gives, on \\([-1,0)\\),\n\\[\ny_n''+\\gamma_ny_n'\n=-\\langle u_n,b_n^{-2}\\Delta_{H_{z,n}}\\varphi\\rangle_2.\n\\]\nThe right side, \\(y_n\\), and \\(\\gamma_n\\) are uniformly bounded. The mean value theorem on \\([-1,-1/2]\\) bounds \\(y_n'\\) at one point, and integration of its first-order equation bounds \\(y_n'\\) throughout \\([-1,0)\\). For smooth boundary data,\n\\[\ny_n(0)=\\langle v_n,\\varphi\\rangle_2;\n\\]\napproximation gives the same identity and Lipschitz bound for \\(L^2\\) data. Taking \\(n\\to\\infty\\), then \\(x\\uparrow0\\), identifies the weak trace as \\(v\\).</p><p>For an eigenvalue \\(\\lambda\\geq0\\) of \\(-\\Delta_{H_\\infty}\\), the radial roots are\n\\[\nd(a^{-2}\\lambda),\\qquad -1-d(a^{-2}\\lambda).\n\\]\nThe latter root is excluded by sectional boundedness as \\(x\\to-\\infty\\), including root \\(-1\\) on constants. The weak boundary trace fixes the remaining coefficients, so\n\\[\nU(x)=\\exp[xd(a^{-2}(-\\Delta_{H_\\infty}))]v.\n\\]\nThus \\(T_{j_n}v_n\\to U(-1)\\) strongly.</p><p>Apply the same compactness and trace argument to\n\\[\nU_n^0(x)=\\exp[xd(b(j_n)^{-2}L(s(j_n)))]v_n.\n\\]\nThese have the same sectional bound, trace limit, and frozen limiting equation. Hence \\(P_{j_n}^0v_n\\) has the same strong limit. Failure of the asserted operator-norm convergence would supply data nearly attaining a fixed positive lower bound, contradicting these limits. Since starts and controls were arbitrary, convergence is uniform.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "After a sufficiently late choice of \\(J\\), for every \\(j\\geq J\\) and \\(i\\leq p\\), \\( \\|(T_j-P_j^*)e_i(s(j+1))\\|_2 \\leq C\\left(\\eta(j)\\log\\frac1{\\eta(j)}+\\eta(j)^2\\right) =o(\\delta_j), \\) uniformly in all controls.", "statement": "<p>**Sharper freezing on the finite smooth frame.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use \\(P_j^*,T_j\\), the reference eigenframe, and \\(\\delta_j=\\eta(j)^{3/4}\\) from Proposition \\(4\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After a sufficiently late choice of \\(J\\), for every \\(j\\geq J\\) and \\(i\\leq p\\),\n\\[\n\\|(T_j-P_j^*)e_i(s(j+1))\\|_2\n\\leq C\\left(\\eta(j)\\log\\frac1{\\eta(j)}+\\eta(j)^2\\right)\n=o(\\delta_j),\n\\]\nuniformly in all controls.</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(\\eta_j=\\eta(j)\\), and set\n\\[\n\\ell_j=4\\log(1/\\eta_j)=3\\log j,\\qquad\n\\varepsilon_j=(1+\\ell_j)\\eta_j.\n\\]\nChoose \\(J\\) so late that \\(j-\\ell_j&gt;0.8J\\) for every \\(j\\geq J\\); it suffices to check this at \\(j=J\\).</p><p>For \\(\\varphi_j=e_i(s(j+1))\\), define the bounded-backward frozen solution\n\\[\nU_j(t)=\n\\exp[(t-j-1)d(b(j)^{-2}(-\\Delta_{H_z(j)}))]\\varphi_j,\n\\qquad t\\leq j+1.\n\\]\nThe finite frame has uniform smooth norms, and frozen metrics lie in a compact smooth family. Applying powers of their Laplacians in the spectral expansion bounds every required spatial and time Sobolev norm. Elliptic estimates and Sobolev embedding give uniform pointwise derivative bounds and \\(\\|U_j(t)\\|_\\infty\\leq C\\).</p><p>On \\([j-\\ell_j,j+1]\\), speeds are comparable to \\(\\eta_j\\), angular coefficients vary from their values at \\(j\\) by \\(O((1+\\ell_j)\\eta_j)\\), and \\(\\gamma-1=O(\\eta_j^2)\\). Substitution into the actual logarithmic equation leaves a residual \\(R_j\\) with\n\\[\n\\|R_j\\|_\\infty\\leq C\\varepsilon_j.\n\\]\nLet \\(u_j\\) be the exact whole-ball extension of \\(\\varphi_j\\), and \\(w_j=u_j-U_j\\). It vanishes at \\(j+1\\), and is uniformly bounded at \\(j-\\ell_j\\): the maximum principle controls \\(u_j\\) throughout its ball independently of the earlier history, while \\(U_j\\) has the preceding bound.</p><p>For late \\(J\\), \\(\\gamma\\geq3/4\\). With \\(K\\) sufficiently large,\n\\[\nB_j(t)=K\\varepsilon_j(j+1-t)\n+K\\exp[-(t-j+\\ell_j)/2]\n\\]\ndominates \\(|w_j|\\) at both endpoints, and\n\\[\n(\\partial_t^2+\\gamma\\partial_t)B_j\n=-K\\gamma\\varepsilon_j\n+K(1/4-\\gamma/2)e^{-(t-j+\\ell_j)/2}\n\\leq-C\\varepsilon_j.\n\\]\nApply the comparison principle to \\(B_j\\pm w_j\\). At \\(t=j\\),\n\\[\n\\|w_j(j)\\|_2\\leq\\|w_j(j)\\|_\\infty\n\\leq C(\\varepsilon_j+e^{-\\ell_j/2})\n=O(\\eta_j\\log(1/\\eta_j)+\\eta_j^2).\n\\]\nDividing by \\(\\delta_j=\\eta_j^{3/4}\\) gives zero in the limit. Only local coefficient bounds enter the residual; the whole-ball maximum principle absorbs the complete earlier history.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "After increasing \\(J\\), there is a fixed \\(\\kappa<1\\) such that, uniformly in controls, \\( T_{QL}=O(\\delta_j),\\qquad T_{LL}=\\operatorname{diag}(\\alpha_i(j)^{-1})+O(\\delta_j), \\) …", "statement": "<p>**Invariant spaces for exact Dirichlet maps; original Proposition \\(5.2\\).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix the program and radial family from Propositions \\(1\\), \\(2\\), and \\(4\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The protected outer gap is\n  \\[\n  \\lambda_{p+1}^{\\mathrm{ord}}(s)-\\max_{i\\leq p}\\lambda_i(s)\n  \\geq g_{\\mathrm{gap}}&gt;0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define the isometry\n  \\[\n  \\iota_j:\\mathbb R^p\\to L^2(\\mu),\\qquad\n  \\iota_jc=\\sum_{i=1}^pc_ie_i(s(j)).\n  \\]\n  Put \\(E_j=\\iota_j\\mathbb R^p\\), let \\(\\Pi_j\\) be its orthogonal projection, and put \\(Q_j=E_j^\\perp\\).</p><p class=\"li\">In the continued frame, write the four blocks of \\(T_j\\) as\n  \\[\n  T_{LL}=\\iota_j^*T_j\\iota_{j+1},\\qquad\n  T_{LQ}=\\iota_j^*T_j|_{Q_{j+1}},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  T_{QL}=(I-\\Pi_j)T_j\\iota_{j+1},\\qquad\n  T_{QQ}=(I-\\Pi_j)T_j|_{Q_{j+1}}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  d_i(j)=d(b(j)^{-2}\\lambda_i(s(j))),\\quad\n  \\alpha_i(j)=e^{d_i(j)},\\quad\n  \\tau_j=\\min_{i\\leq p}\\alpha_i(j)^{-1}.\n  \\]\n  Since the finite program is fixed,\n  \\[\n  \\tau_j\\geq\\tau_*:=\n  \\inf_{\\substack{s\\geq0,\\ b\\in[a,1]\\\\i\\leq p}}\n  e^{-d(b^{-2}\\lambda_i(s))}&gt;0.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After increasing \\(J\\), there is a fixed \\(\\kappa&lt;1\\) such that, uniformly in controls,\n\\[\nT_{QL}=O(\\delta_j),\\qquad\nT_{LL}=\\operatorname{diag}(\\alpha_i(j)^{-1})+O(\\delta_j),\n\\]\n\\[\nT_{LQ}=o(1),\\qquad \\|T_{QQ}\\|\\leq\\kappa\\tau_j.\n\\]\nThere is a unique family \\(V_j:\\mathbb R^p\\to Q_j\\), among families in one fixed sufficiently small operator ball, such that \\(T_j\\) maps\n\\[\n\\operatorname{graph}_{j+1}V_{j+1}\n=\\{\\iota_{j+1}c+V_{j+1}c:c\\in\\mathbb R^p\\}\n\\]\nisomorphically onto \\(\\operatorname{graph}_jV_j\\). Moreover,\n\\[\n\\|V_j\\|\\leq C\\delta_j.\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(j\\), \\(V_j\\) is operator-norm continuous in the complete control sequence with its countable product topology.</p><p>The induced outward matrix is\n\\[\nA_j=(T_{LL}+T_{LQ}V_{j+1})^{-1}\n=\\operatorname{diag}(\\alpha_i(j))+O(\\delta_j),\n\\]\nwith\n\\[\nT_{LQ}V_{j+1}=o(\\delta_j).\n\\]\nIt is also continuous in the product topology.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma \\(5.1\\) gives the block estimates. Lemma \\(5.2\\) gives the unique invariant graph family and its exact transfer identity, and Lemma \\(5.3\\) gives product-topology continuity.</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\nT_{LQ}V_{j+1}=o(1)O(\\delta_{j+1})=o(\\delta_j).\n\\]\nInverting the finite low matrix gives\n\\[\nA_j=M_j(V_{j+1})^{-1}\n=\\operatorname{diag}(\\alpha_i(j))+O(\\delta_j),\n\\]\nwith the stated continuity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "After a sufficiently late start, the four block estimates in Proposition \\(5\\) hold uniformly over all controls.", "statement": "<p>**Low/high block estimates.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use all notation and the fixed outer spectral gap of Proposition \\(5\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After a sufficiently late start, the four block estimates in Proposition \\(5\\) hold uniformly over all controls.</p>", "proof": "<p>The frame moves by \\(O(\\eta(j))=o(\\delta_j)\\) in one step. The first-\\(p\\) projection \\(\\Pi_j^*\\) for the controlled link \\(H_z(j)\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\|\\Pi_j^*-\\Pi_j\\|=O(\\delta_j)\n\\]\nby the isolated-cluster projection formula and the outer gap. Internal doubles cause no loss because this concerns the entire cluster.</p><p>The frozen \\(P_j^*\\) preserves \\(\\Pi_j^*\\), so its action on the reference frame has high component \\(O(\\delta_j)\\). Smooth finite-cluster functional calculus gives its low block as\n\\[\n\\operatorname{diag}(\\alpha_i(j)^{-1})+O(\\delta_j).\n\\]\nThe finite-frame estimate in Proposition \\(4\\), together with frame movement, gives the two asserted blocks on low inputs.</p><p>For the other two blocks, use the full operator estimate from Proposition \\(4\\). The reference \\(P_j^0\\) preserves \\(E_j,Q_j\\), while replacing \\(Q_{j+1}\\) by \\(Q_j\\) costs \\(O(\\eta(j))\\). Thus \\(T_{LQ}=o(1)\\).</p><p>Strict monotonicity of \\(d\\), the outer gap, and compactness of phase and \\(b\\in[a,1]\\) give \\(\\kappa_{\\mathrm{ref}}&lt;1\\) with\n\\[\n\\|P_j^0|_{Q_j}\\|\\leq\\kappa_{\\mathrm{ref}}\\tau_j.\n\\]\nChoose \\(\\kappa_{\\mathrm{ref}}&lt;\\kappa&lt;1\\). Since \\(\\tau_j\\geq\\tau_*&gt;0\\), the absolute \\(o(1)\\) freezing and frame errors are absorbed into\n\\[\n(\\kappa-\\kappa_{\\mathrm{ref}})\\tau_j.\n\\]\nFixing \\(p\\) before \\(J\\) is essential here.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "On one fixed sufficiently small operator ball, and for sufficiently late \\(J\\), every \\(\\mathcal F_j\\) is defined, maps that ball into itself, and has Lipschitz constant at most a common \\(\\kappa_0<1\\).", "statement": "<p>**Contracting graph transform.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the block bounds of Lemma \\(5.1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(V:\\mathbb R^p\\to Q_{j+1}\\), define\n  \\[\n  M_j(V)=T_{LL}+T_{LQ}V,\\qquad\n  \\mathcal F_j(V)=(T_{QL}+T_{QQ}V)M_j(V)^{-1}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>On one fixed sufficiently small operator ball, and for sufficiently late \\(J\\), every \\(\\mathcal F_j\\) is defined, maps that ball into itself, and has Lipschitz constant at most a common \\(\\kappa_0&lt;1\\).</p><p>Inward iteration from any bounded terminal graphs produces the unique graph family of Proposition \\(5\\), and\n\\[\n\\|V_j\\|\\leq C\\delta_j.\n\\]\nThe exact identity is\n\\[\nT_j(\\iota_{j+1}c+V_{j+1}c)\n=\n\\iota_jM_j(V_{j+1})c+V_jM_j(V_{j+1})c.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(D_j=\\operatorname{diag}(\\alpha_i(j)^{-1})\\). On a fixed small ball,\n\\[\n\\|D_j^{-1}(M_j(V)-D_j)\\|\n\\leq\\tau_*^{-1}\\bigl(O(\\delta_j)+o(1)\\|V\\|\\bigr)\\to0.\n\\]\nHence \\(M_j(V)\\) is invertible and\n\\[\n\\|M_j(V)^{-1}\\|\\leq\\tau_j^{-1}(1+o(1)).\n\\]\nIn particular, \\(\\|\\mathcal F_j(0)\\|\\leq C\\delta_j\\).</p><p>The inverse-difference identity gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal F_j(V)-\\mathcal F_j(W)\n={}&amp;T_{QQ}(V-W)M_j(V)^{-1}\\\\\n&amp;+(T_{QL}+T_{QQ}W)\n\\bigl(M_j(V)^{-1}-M_j(W)^{-1}\\bigr),\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nM_j(V)^{-1}-M_j(W)^{-1}\n=-M_j(V)^{-1}T_{LQ}(V-W)M_j(W)^{-1}.\n\\]\nThe first term has Lipschitz constant \\(\\kappa+o(1)\\); the second has constant \\(o(1)\\), since the ball is fixed, \\(\\tau_*&gt;0\\), and \\(T_{LQ}=o(1)\\). Choose \\(\\kappa&lt;\\kappa_0&lt;1\\), then increase \\(J\\) so the Lipschitz constants are at most \\(\\kappa_0\\) and \\(\\|\\mathcal F_j(0)\\|\\) is smaller than \\((1-\\kappa_0)\\) times the ball radius.</p><p>For an outer cut \\(R\\), set \\(V_R^{[R]}=0\\), and iterate inward:\n\\[\nV_j^{[R]}=\\mathcal F_j(V_{j+1}^{[R]}).\n\\]\nTwo terminal choices differ at fixed \\(j\\) by at most \\(C\\kappa_0^{R-j}\\), uniformly in controls. Thus the iterates converge uniformly to \\(V_j\\), with\n\\[\nV_j=\\mathcal F_j(V_{j+1}),\\qquad\n\\|V_j\\|\\leq C\\sum_{m\\geq0}\\kappa_0^m\\delta_{j+m}\n\\leq C'\\delta_j,\n\\]\nbecause \\(\\delta_j\\) decreases. Contraction also proves uniqueness.</p><p>The fixed graph equation is precisely the displayed transfer identity. Invertibility of \\(M_j(V_{j+1})\\) gives the graph isomorphism.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every fixed \\(j\\), \\(V_j\\) and \\(A_j\\) are operator-norm continuous in the control product topology.", "statement": "<p>**Continuity in the complete control sequence.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the exact maps, graph transforms, and invariant family of Proposition \\(5\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every fixed \\(j\\), \\(V_j\\) and \\(A_j\\) are operator-norm continuous in the control product topology.</p>", "proof": "<p>For fixed \\(\\ell\\), the metric on the ball of radius \\(e^{\\ell+1}\\) depends on only finitely many controls. For smooth boundary data, choose a fixed smooth extension into the ball. For \\(0&lt;\\alpha&lt;1\\), let\n\\[\nX=\\{v\\in C^{2,\\alpha}(B):v|_{\\partial B}=0\\}.\n\\]\nThe remaining Dirichlet operator\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_z:X\\to C^\\alpha(B)\n\\]\nis bijective by the classical Dirichlet theorem and has bounded inverse. Convergent controls give operator-norm convergence between these Hölder spaces. A Neumann series and the inverse-difference identity give convergence of solutions and inner traces. Smooth approximation and the contraction bound therefore imply\n\\[\nT_\\ell(z_n)v\\to T_\\ell(z)v\n\\quad\\text{in }L^2\n\\]\nfor every fixed \\(v\\in L^2\\).</p><p>If \\(V_n:\\mathbb R^p\\to Q_{\\ell+1}\\) converge in operator norm to \\(V\\), then for each coordinate basis vector \\(e\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|T_\\ell(z_n)V_ne-T_\\ell(z)Ve\\|_2\n&amp;\\leq\\|V_ne-Ve\\|_2\n+\\|(T_\\ell(z_n)-T_\\ell(z))Ve\\|_2\\\\\n&amp;\\to0.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe same holds on fixed low basis vectors \\(\\iota_{\\ell+1}e\\). A finite basis gives precisely the finite-domain operator-norm continuity needed in \\(\\mathcal F_\\ell\\). The low inverses are finite matrices with uniform inverse bounds, so inversion is continuous.</p><p>Each finite inward graph iterate is consequently continuous in finitely many controls. Its error from \\(V_j\\) is at most \\(C\\kappa_0^{R-j}\\), uniformly over the complete control product. First choose \\(R\\) to control this tail, then control the finitely many relevant coordinates. This proves continuity of \\(V_j\\). The same finite-rank estimate and finite-matrix inversion prove continuity of \\(A_j\\). No full operator-norm continuity of \\(T_j\\) on the infinite-dimensional space is asserted.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every mesh point whose phase lies on that plateau, \\( (A_j)_{hi} =z_\\nu\\eta_\\nu^{3/4}m_{hi}(s(j),b(j))+o(\\delta_j), \\) uniformly over all controls, past and future.", "statement": "<p>**Signed off-diagonal entry; original Proposition \\(5.3\\).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the fixed angular program, controlled metrics, and exact outward matrices \\(A_j\\) of Propositions \\(1\\), \\(2\\), and \\(5\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose reference labels \\(i\\neq h\\) have an isolated linearly split double at \\(s_\\nu\\), with positive eigenvalue \\(\\lambda\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(L(w)=-\\Delta_{h(w)}\\), let\n  \\[\n  \\dot L_\\nu(s)=\n  \\left.\\frac{d}{d\\varepsilon}\\right|_{\\varepsilon=0}\n  L(w(s)+\\varepsilon\\psi_\\nu),\n  \\]\n  where\n  \\[\n  \\langle e_h(s_\\nu),\\dot L_\\nu(s_\\nu)e_i(s_\\nu)\\rangle_2\\neq0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\eta_\\nu=\\eta(t_\\nu)\\), \\(s(t_\\nu)=s_\\nu\\), and choose the bump plateau in a sufficiently small fixed neighborhood of this crossing.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every mesh point whose phase lies on that plateau,\n\\[\n(A_j)_{hi}\n=z_\\nu\\eta_\\nu^{3/4}m_{hi}(s(j),b(j))+o(\\delta_j),\n\\]\nuniformly over all controls, past and future.</p><p>The coefficient \\(m_{hi}\\) is continuous, has a fixed nonzero sign, and is bounded away from zero on the chosen neighborhood for \\(b\\) near \\(a\\). At the crossing, with \\(\\beta=b^{-2}\\) and \\(\\alpha=e^{d(\\beta\\lambda)}\\),\n\\[\nm_{hi}(s_\\nu,b)\n=\n\\alpha\\beta d'(\\beta\\lambda)\n\\langle e_h(s_\\nu),\\dot L_\\nu(s_\\nu)e_i(s_\\nu)\\rangle_2.\n\\]\nThe reversed entry has the same nonzero coefficient at the crossing.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix a mesh point on the plateau and write\n\\[\n\\beta=b(j)^{-2},\\qquad \\varepsilon=z_\\nu\\eta_\\nu^{3/4},\\qquad\nL_\\varepsilon=L(w(s(j))+\\varepsilon\\psi_\\nu).\n\\]\nDefine\n\\[\nq_\\beta(x)=e^{d(\\beta x)},\\qquad p_\\beta(x)=q_\\beta(x)^{-1}.\n\\]\nLet \\(\\Pi_\\varepsilon\\) be the isolated first-\\(p\\) projection, \\(\\Pi=\\Pi_0\\), and \\(E=\\Pi L^2\\). Compress the frozen inward map:\n\\[\nC_-(\\varepsilon)=\\Pi p_\\beta(L_\\varepsilon)\\Pi|_E.\n\\]\nIts part through \\(I-\\Pi_\\varepsilon\\) is \\(O(\\varepsilon^2)\\): each input/output projection has size \\(O(\\varepsilon)\\), while \\(p_\\beta(L_\\varepsilon)\\) is a contraction. The remaining part is a smooth finite-cluster matrix. Differentiation in the reference eigenbasis gives\n\\[\n(C_-'(0))_{hi}\n=\np_\\beta[\\lambda_h(s(j)),\\lambda_i(s(j))]\n\\langle e_h(s(j)),\\dot L_\\nu(s(j))e_i(s(j))\\rangle_2,\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nq[x,y]=\\frac{q(x)-q(y)}{x-y},\\qquad q[x,x]=q'(x).\n\\]\nThis is the derivative of the full isolated cluster, including at a double.</p><p>At zero, \\(C_-(0)\\) is diagonal. Differentiate its inverse and use\n\\[\n-q_\\beta(x)q_\\beta(y)p_\\beta[x,y]=q_\\beta[x,y].\n\\]\nThe off-diagonal derivative of \\(C_-(\\varepsilon)^{-1}\\) is therefore\n\\[\nq_\\beta[\\lambda_h(s(j)),\\lambda_i(s(j))]\n\\langle e_h(s(j)),\\dot L_\\nu(s(j))e_i(s(j))\\rangle_2.\n\\]\nThis defines \\(m_{hi}(s(j),b(j))\\). At equality,\n\\[\nq_\\beta'(\\lambda)=e^{d(\\beta\\lambda)}\\beta d'(\\beta\\lambda),\n\\]\ngiving the stated formula. The factor \\(\\beta\\) occurs exactly once. Continuity of divided differences and the nonzero angular entry give a fixed sign and positive lower bound after shrinking the neighborhood. Self-adjointness of \\(\\dot L_\\nu\\) makes the reversed entry equal at the crossing.</p><p>The finite-cluster Taylor remainder is \\(O(\\varepsilon^2)\\). Replacing the frame at \\(j+1\\) by that at \\(j\\) costs \\(O(\\eta(j))\\). Proposition \\(4\\) replaces the frozen map on that frame by \\(T_j\\) with \\(o(\\delta_j)\\) error; Proposition \\(5\\) contributes only\n\\[\nT_{LQ}V_{j+1}=o(\\delta_j).\n\\]\nFinite inverses have uniform bounds depending on fixed \\(\\tau_*&gt;0\\), so inversion preserves these error orders. A fixed phase neighborhood has time width \\(O(\\eta_\\nu^{-1})=o(t_\\nu)\\), hence \\(\\eta(j)/\\eta_\\nu\\to1\\) uniformly as the start increases. Thus\n\\[\nO(\\varepsilon^2)=O(\\delta_j^2)=o(\\delta_j).\n\\]\nAll remainders are uniform in other controls by the whole-ball freezing and future-graph bounds.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For sufficiently late \\(J\\), one control sequence \\(z\\) can be chosen so that there are columns \\( v_i(j)=\\mathbf e_i+\\sum_{h\\neq i}u_{hi}(j)\\mathbf e_h \\qquad(j\\geq J) \\) with …", "statement": "<p>**Simultaneous scalar matching; original Section \\(6\\), including Lemma \\(6.1\\).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix the angular program and radial family, with \\(k,p\\) fixed before the start \\(J\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(P_{\\mathrm{lab}}=NS\\) and\n  \\[\n  \\Theta_i(s)=d(a^{-2}\\lambda_i(s)),\\qquad\n  m_i=\\frac1{P_{\\mathrm{lab}}}\\int_0^{P_{\\mathrm{lab}}}\\Theta_i(s)\\,ds&lt;k.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Use the exact matrices from Proposition \\(5\\):\n  \\[\n  A_j=\\operatorname{diag}(\\alpha_1(j),\\ldots,\\alpha_p(j))+\\mathcal E_j,\n  \\qquad \\|\\mathcal E_j\\|\\leq C\\delta_j.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Use the fixed disjoint crossing bumps of Proposition \\(2\\), chosen as in Proposition \\(6\\), with\n  \\[\n  (\\mathcal E_j)_{hi}\n  =z_\\nu\\eta_\\nu^{3/4}m_{hi}(s(j),b(j))+o(\\delta_j)\n  \\]\n  on the crossing plateau, and \\(|m_{hi}|\\geq c_0&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathbf e_i\\) denote the \\(i\\)-th coordinate vector of \\(\\mathbb R^p\\), distinct from the angular function \\(e_i(s)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For sufficiently late \\(J\\), one control sequence \\(z\\) can be chosen so that there are columns\n\\[\nv_i(j)=\\mathbf e_i+\\sum_{h\\neq i}u_{hi}(j)\\mathbf e_h\n\\qquad(j\\geq J)\n\\]\nwith\n\\[\n|u_{hi}(j)|\\leq C\\eta(j)^{1/4}&lt;\\eta(j)^{1/8},\n\\]\nand positive multipliers\n\\[\n\\sigma_i(j)=(A_jv_i(j))_i=\\alpha_i(j)+O(\\delta_j)&gt;0,\n\\]\nsatisfying\n\\[\nA_jv_i(j)=\\sigma_i(j)v_i(j+1).\n\\]\nAt every step the \\(p\\) columns form a basis. The same control sequence works for all labels.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(\\mathcal X\\) be the countable product of all coordinate and control intervals\n\\[\n|u_{hi}(j)|\\leq\\eta(j)^{1/8},\\qquad |z_\\nu|\\leq1,\n\\]\nwith the product topology.</p><p>Given a point of \\(\\mathcal X\\), compute forces using Lemma \\(7.1\\), then all stable trial propagations. At a two-incoming-value cut, use the forward value as the output coordinate. For controls use\n\\[\nz_\\nu\\longmapsto\\Pi_{[-1,1]}(z_\\nu-D_\\nu).\n\\]\nLemma \\(7.2\\) puts each trial coordinate strictly inside its interval; projection keeps each control in its interval. These rules define a self-map \\(\\Phi:\\mathcal X\\to\\mathcal X\\).</p><p>The map is continuous. For fixed \\(j\\), \\(A_j\\) is continuous in the control product by Proposition \\(5\\), and each force depends on finitely many column coordinates through a denominator bounded away from zero. Finite propagations and mismatches are finite expressions in those forces. The only infinite case is backward propagation for a noncrossing pair, whose geometric tail is uniformly summable. Thus every output coordinate is continuous.</p><p>Enumerate the coordinates of \\(\\mathcal X\\). Freeze coordinates after the first \\(R\\) at their box centers, and apply finite-dimensional Brouwer to the first \\(R\\) output coordinates. This gives \\(x^{[R]}\\) satisfying its first \\(R\\) fixed-point equations. Repeated subsequence extraction in compact coordinate intervals gives a subsequence converging in every coordinate to \\(x\\in\\mathcal X\\). Each fixed coordinate equation holds eventually and passes to the limit by continuity. Hence \\(\\Phi(x)=x\\).</p><p>No fixed control equals \\(1\\): Lemma \\(7.3\\) gives \\(1-D_\\nu&lt;1\\), whose projection is less than \\(1\\). Similarly, a control equal to \\(-1\\) would have projected image greater than \\(-1\\). All controls are interior, and\n\\[\nz_\\nu=\\Pi_{[-1,1]}(z_\\nu-D_\\nu)\n\\]\ntherefore implies \\(D_\\nu=0\\). Forward and backward values match at every incoming cut. Elsewhere the propagations already obey the recurrence, including prescribed zero cuts. Thus every scalar recurrence holds exactly.</p><p>Fix this single control sequence. Set\n\\[\n\\sigma_i(j)=(A_jv_i(j))_i\n=\\alpha_i(j)+O(\\delta_j)&gt;0.\n\\]\nThe recurrences give\n\\[\nA_jv_i(j)=\\sigma_i(j)v_i(j+1).\n\\]\nLemma \\(7.2\\) gives the stronger \\(C\\eta^{1/4}\\)-coordinate bound. The column matrix is the identity plus a matrix of norm\n\\[\nO(p\\eta(j)^{1/8})&lt;1\n\\]\nfor late \\(J\\). Hence the columns form a basis at every step. The chosen metric and controls are common to all labels.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The line invariance condition is exactly \\( u_{hi}(j+1)=r_{hi}(j)u_{hi}(j)+F_{hi}(j). \\) Uniformly on the whole product of boxes, \\( |F_{hi}(j)|\\leq C_1\\delta_j. \\) At an endpoint choice \\(z_\\nu=\\pm1\\), on that pair’s …", "statement": "<p>**Scalar recurrences and one obstruction per crossing.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition \\(7\\), but initially let all controls and all coordinates vary independently in\n  \\[\n  |u_{hi}(j)|\\leq\\eta(j)^{1/8},\\qquad |z_\\nu|\\leq1.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  r_{hi}(j)=\\frac{\\alpha_h(j)}{\\alpha_i(j)},\\qquad\n  F_{hi}(j)=\n  \\frac{(A_jv_i(j))_h}{(A_jv_i(j))_i}\n  -r_{hi}(j)u_{hi}(j).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">At crossing \\(\\nu\\), cut the mesh at \\(N_\\nu=\\lceil t_\\nu\\rceil\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The line invariance condition is exactly\n\\[\nu_{hi}(j+1)=r_{hi}(j)u_{hi}(j)+F_{hi}(j).\n\\]\nUniformly on the whole product of boxes,\n\\[\n|F_{hi}(j)|\\leq C_1\\delta_j.\n\\]\nAt an endpoint choice \\(z_\\nu=\\pm1\\), on that pair’s crossing plateau,\n\\[\nF_{hi}(j)=\nz_\\nu\\eta_\\nu^{3/4}\n\\frac{m_{hi}(s(j),b(j))}{\\alpha_i(j)}\n+o(\\delta_j).\n\\]\nAlso,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{sign}\\log r_{hi}(j)\n=\\operatorname{sign}(\\lambda_h(s(j))-\\lambda_i(s(j))).\n\\]</p><p>A positive-to-negative sign change is a zero cut, where the trial value is prescribed to be zero and stable propagation goes away from the cut. A negative-to-positive sign change has two incoming stable propagations and gives exactly one scalar mismatch condition per double crossing.</p><p>If \\(a_0&lt;N&lt;b_0\\) are adjacent zero cuts for an ordered pair, the incoming values at the negative-to-positive cut \\(N\\) are\n\\[\nu_N^-=\\sum_{q=a_0}^{N-1}F_{hi}(q)\n\\prod_{\\ell=q+1}^{N-1}r_{hi}(\\ell),\n\\]\n\\[\nu_N^+=-\\sum_{q=N}^{b_0-1}F_{hi}(q)\n\\prod_{\\ell=N}^{q}r_{hi}(\\ell)^{-1}.\n\\]\nTheir mismatch is the positive-weight sum\n\\[\nD_\\nu=u_N^--u_N^+\n=\n\\sum_{q=a_0}^{N-1}W_{q,N}^-F_{hi}(q)\n+\\sum_{q=N}^{b_0-1}W_{N,q}^+F_{hi}(q),\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nW_{q,N}^-=\\prod_{\\ell=q+1}^{N-1}r_{hi}(\\ell)&gt;0,\\qquad\nW_{N,q}^+=\\prod_{\\ell=N}^{q}r_{hi}(\\ell)^{-1}&gt;0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For late \\(J\\), the denominator\n\\[\n(A_jv_i(j))_i=\\alpha_i(j)+O(\\delta_j)\n\\]\nis positive throughout the boxes. Division by that coordinate makes the displayed recurrence exactly equivalent to line invariance.</p><p>Suppress \\(j\\), and write \\(\\mathcal E_{h\\ell}=(\\mathcal E_j)_{h\\ell}\\). Direct expansion gives\n\\[\nF_{hi}=\n\\frac{\n\\mathcal E_{hi}\n+\\sum_{\\ell\\neq i}\\mathcal E_{h\\ell}u_{\\ell i}\n-r_{hi}u_{hi}\n\\left(\\mathcal E_{ii}\n+\\sum_{\\ell\\neq i}\\mathcal E_{i\\ell}u_{\\ell i}\\right)\n}{\n\\alpha_i+\\mathcal E_{ii}\n+\\sum_{\\ell\\neq i}\\mathcal E_{i\\ell}u_{\\ell i}\n}.\n\\]\nThe \\(\\alpha_i\\) and their ratios lie in a fixed compact positive interval because \\(p\\) is fixed. This proves \\(|F_{hi}|\\leq C_1\\delta_j\\), using the exact cancellation of the reference diagonal matrix.</p><p>On a crossing plateau, Proposition \\(6\\) gives the leading entry. Every numerator term involving a coordinate is\n\\[\nO(\\delta_j\\eta(j)^{1/8})=o(\\delta_j),\n\\]\nand replacing the denominator by \\(\\alpha_i(j)\\) costs \\(O(\\delta_j^2)\\). Since \\(\\eta(j)/\\eta_\\nu\\to1\\) uniformly there, endpoint choices have a strict fixed sign and magnitude at least \\(c_1\\eta_\\nu^{3/4}\\) after a late start, independently of all other variables.</p><p>Strict monotonicity of \\(d\\) and the common factor \\(b(j)^{-2}\\) prove the ratio-sign identity. The zeros are simple in phase. For \\(j&lt;N_\\nu\\), the step begins before the crossing; for \\(j\\geq N_\\nu\\), it begins at or after it. If \\(t_\\nu\\) is an integer, the step beginning there has ratio \\(1\\); the subsequent estimates allow this neutral step.</p><p>When \\(r_{hi}&lt;1\\), the scalar recurrence is stable forward; when \\(r_{hi}&gt;1\\), it is stable backward. Thus\n\\[\n+\\longrightarrow-:\\quad \\leftarrow N\\rightarrow,\\quad u_{hi}(N)=0,\n\\]\nwhereas\n\\[\n-\\longrightarrow+:\\quad \\rightarrow N\\leftarrow\n\\]\nrequires matching two incoming values. Reversing the ordered pair replaces \\(r_{hi}\\) by \\(r_{hi}^{-1}\\), interchanging these roles. Hence each double produces exactly one matching condition.</p><p>Iterating the scalar recurrence from neighboring zero cuts gives the stated forward and backward formulas. The minus sign in backward propagation makes both weight families positive in \\(D_\\nu\\).</p><p>For an initially forward-stable interval, use \\(a_0=J\\) and zero trial value there. If initially backward stable, propagate backward from its first zero cut. A pair that never crosses has a uniform strict ratio gap; use zero at \\(J\\) in the forward-stable case and the convergent backward sum from infinity in the backward-stable case. A crossing pair has infinitely many alternating cuts with bounded phase gaps, so all its remaining propagations are finite.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For sufficiently late \\(J\\), every trial coordinate satisfies, uniformly on the whole product, \\( |u_{hi}^{\\mathrm{trial}}(j)| \\leq C\\eta(j)^{1/4}<\\eta(j)^{1/8}. \\) On a switching interval with comparable phase speed …", "statement": "<p>**Stable-weight and coordinate bounds; first part of original Lemma \\(6.1\\).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the trial propagations and full product of boxes from Lemma \\(7.1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For sufficiently late \\(J\\), every trial coordinate satisfies, uniformly on the whole product,\n\\[\n|u_{hi}^{\\mathrm{trial}}(j)|\n\\leq C\\eta(j)^{1/4}&lt;\\eta(j)^{1/8}.\n\\]\nOn a switching interval with comparable phase speed \\(\\eta_*\\), stable products of \\(m\\) steps satisfy\n\\[\nW(m)\\leq Ce^{-c_*\\eta_*m^2},\\qquad\n\\sum_{m\\geq0}W(m)\\leq C\\eta_*^{-1/2}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For a fixed ordered pair define\n\\[\n\\ell_{hi}(s,b)=d(b^{-2}\\lambda_h(s))-d(b^{-2}\\lambda_i(s)).\n\\]\nOn a sign interval \\([s_a,s_b]\\) bounded by successive zeros, simple splitting and compactness of finitely repeated types give\n\\[\n|\\ell_{hi}(s,b)|\n\\geq c_*\\min\\{s-s_a,s_b-s,1\\},\n\\tag{6.17}\n\\]\nuniformly for \\(b\\) near \\(a\\). Near either endpoint there is also a linear upper bound. The common radial factor does not change the zeros, and \\(d'\\) has positive upper and lower bounds on the finite spectral range. Interval phase lengths have positive lower and finite upper bounds. Initial intervals are truncated sign intervals after periodic extension for this comparison.</p><p>Across a bounded phase interval, elapsed time is \\(O(t^{3/4})=o(t)\\), so speeds are comparable to a single \\(\\eta_*\\), with relative error tending to zero as the start increases. Consecutive mesh phases are separated by comparable multiples of \\(\\eta_*\\).</p><p>Consider a stable product of \\(m\\) steps. Apart from bounded endpoint-rounding steps, a fixed fraction of its phases have distance at least \\(c_*\\eta_*m\\) from both endpoints: phases within distance \\(d\\) of the two endpoints number at most \\(C(d/\\eta_*+1)\\), so take \\(d=c_*'\\eta_*m\\) with \\(c_*'\\) small. Since \\(\\eta_*m\\) is bounded by the maximum phase length, summing (6.17) gives\n\\[\n\\sum|\\log r_{hi}|\\geq c_*\\eta_*m^2-C.\n\\]\nStable direction exponentiates the negative of this sum. Ceiling conventions and a possible neutral step only alter \\(C\\). Hence\n\\[\nW(m)\\leq Ce^{-c_*\\eta_*m^2},\\qquad\n\\sum_{m\\geq0}W(m)\\leq C\\eta_*^{-1/2}\n\\]\nby Gaussian integral comparison.</p><p>On a switching interval, \\(\\delta_q\\) is comparable to \\(\\eta_*^{3/4}\\). The force bound from Lemma \\(7.1\\) and the Green sum give\n\\[\n|u^{\\mathrm{trial}}|\\leq\nC\\eta_*^{3/4}\\eta_*^{-1/2}\n=C\\eta_*^{1/4},\n\\]\ncomparable to \\(C\\eta(j)^{1/4}\\) at the output.</p><p>For a noncrossing pair, compactness gives a fixed \\(\\rho&lt;1\\) with either \\(r_{hi}\\leq\\rho\\) or \\(r_{hi}^{-1}\\leq\\rho\\) at every step. The backward tail is bounded by\n\\[\n\\sum_{q=j}^\\infty C\\rho^{q-j}\\delta_q\\leq C'\\delta_j.\n\\]\nFor a forward sum use\n\\[\n\\frac{\\delta_q}{\\delta_j}\n=(j/q)^{9/16}\\leq(1+j-q)^{9/16},\n\\]\nto obtain\n\\[\n\\sum_{q=J}^{j-1}\\rho^{j-1-q}\\delta_q\n\\leq\\delta_j\\sum_{m\\geq0}\\rho^m(m+2)^{9/16}\n\\leq C'\\delta_j.\n\\]\nThis is also at most \\(C\\eta(j)^{1/4}\\). Finally, \\(C\\eta^{1/4}&lt;\\eta^{1/8}\\) for a sufficiently late start.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Orient each control coordinate \\(z_\\nu\\) according to its selected coefficient.", "statement": "<p>**Endpoint signs of the mismatches; second part of original Lemma \\(6.1\\).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the mismatches \\(D_\\nu\\), crossing plateaux, and independent boxes of Lemmas \\(7.1\\) and \\(7.2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Orient each control coordinate \\(z_\\nu\\) according to its selected coefficient. For sufficiently late \\(J\\),\n\\[\nD_\\nu|_{z_\\nu=1}\\geq c_*\\eta_\\nu^{1/4}&gt;0,\n\\qquad\nD_\\nu|_{z_\\nu=-1}\\leq-c_*\\eta_\\nu^{1/4}&lt;0,\n\\]\nindependently of every other control and column coordinate.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix a small \\(\\theta&gt;0\\), and consider the mesh window\n\\[\n|j-t_\\nu|\\leq\\theta\\eta_\\nu^{-1/2}.\n\\]\nIts phase width is \\(O(\\theta\\eta_\\nu^{1/2})\\), so it lies inside the fixed plateau for a late start. The linear upper bound on the ratio gap shows every incoming weight from that window is at least \\(e^{-C\\theta^2}\\): the logarithmic loss is at most\n\\[\nC\\eta_\\nu\\sum_{m\\leq\\theta\\eta_\\nu^{-1/2}}(m+1).\n\\]\nThe incoming sides together contain at least \\(c_*\\eta_\\nu^{-1/2}\\) indices. At \\(z_\\nu=\\pm1\\), Lemma \\(7.1\\) gives all their forces the selected sign and magnitude at least \\(c_1\\eta_\\nu^{3/4}\\). Thus their contribution has that sign and magnitude at least \\(c_2\\eta_\\nu^{1/4}\\).</p><p>Every other term on the plateau has the same sign. Outside the fixed plateau, phase distance from the crossing is bounded below, so an incoming weight contains a stable segment of length \\(c_*\\eta_\\nu^{-1}\\). Lemma \\(7.2\\) bounds it by\n\\[\nCe^{-c_*'/\\eta_\\nu}.\n\\]\nThe two neighboring switching intervals contain at most \\(C\\eta_\\nu^{-1}\\) terms with forces \\(O(\\eta_\\nu^{3/4})\\). Their total contribution outside the plateau is at most\n\\[\nC\\eta_\\nu^{-1/4}e^{-c_*'/\\eta_\\nu}\n=o(\\eta_\\nu^{1/4}).\n\\]\nFor a truncated first interval, separation of the start from the first crossing places the central window after \\(J\\). Deleting earlier terms does not alter the signed lower bound, and the retained tails have the same estimate. The central window dominates.</p><p>Reverse \\(z_\\nu\\) if necessary so its positive endpoint gives positive mismatch. Uniformity of the force expansion over all other variables proves the assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are \\(p\\) independent smooth entire real harmonic functions \\(U_1,\\ldots,U_p\\) on \\((\\mathbb R^3,g)\\) whose traces at radius \\(e^j\\) are \\(g_i(j)\\).", "statement": "<p>**Entire harmonic functions and growth at every radius; original Section \\(7\\).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix the single control sequence from Proposition \\(7\\), and write \\(g=g_z\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use its columns \\(v_i(j)\\), multipliers \\(\\sigma_i(j)\\), graph maps \\(V_j\\), and frame maps \\(\\iota_j\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  w_i(j)=\\iota_jv_i(j)+V_jv_i(j),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  c_i(J)=1,\\qquad\n  c_i(j)=\\prod_{\\ell=J}^{j-1}\\sigma_i(\\ell),\\qquad\n  g_i(j)=c_i(j)w_i(j).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  \\Theta_i(s)=d(a^{-2}\\lambda_i(s)),\\qquad\n  m_i=\\frac1{P_{\\mathrm{lab}}}\\int_0^{P_{\\mathrm{lab}}}\\Theta_i(s)\\,ds&lt;k.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are \\(p\\) independent smooth entire real harmonic functions \\(U_1,\\ldots,U_p\\) on \\((\\mathbb R^3,g)\\) whose traces at radius \\(e^j\\) are \\(g_i(j)\\).</p><p>For every \\(i\\),\n\\[\n\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}\\frac1t\\log\\|U_i(t,\\cdot)\\|_{L^2(\\mu)}=m_i.\n\\]\nFor one common number \\(\\gamma_*\\) with\n\\[\n\\max_{i\\leq p}m_i&lt;\\gamma_*&lt;k,\n\\]\nthe functions satisfy\n\\[\n\\|U_i(t,\\cdot)\\|_2\\leq C_i e^{\\gamma_*t}\n\\]\non every sufficiently large sphere, and\n\\[\n|U_i(x)|\\leq C_i'(1+d_g(0,x))^k\n\\qquad(x\\in\\mathbb R^3).\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\nh_k(\\mathbb R^3,g)\\geq p=(\\lfloor\\vartheta k\\rfloor+1)^2.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma \\(8.1\\) constructs the independent entire harmonic functions from compatible whole-ball traces. Lemma \\(8.2\\) proves their exact spherical exponents, controls every intermediate sphere, and converts those estimates to pointwise degree-\\(k\\) growth. Hence all \\(p\\) functions belong to \\(\\mathcal H_k(\\mathbb R^3,g)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The traces satisfy \\( T_jg_i(j+1)=g_i(j). \\) Each \\(g_i(j)\\) is smooth.", "statement": "<p>**Compatible traces and entire functions; original Section \\(7.1\\).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the graph-lifted traces \\(w_i(j),g_i(j)\\) in Proposition \\(8\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The traces satisfy\n\\[\nT_jg_i(j+1)=g_i(j).\n\\]\nEach \\(g_i(j)\\) is smooth. Their harmonic extensions to nested whole balls agree on overlaps and define \\(p\\) independent smooth entire harmonic functions.</p>", "proof": "<p>The exact graph identity of Proposition \\(5\\), together with\n\\[\nA_jv_i(j)=\\sigma_i(j)v_i(j+1),\n\\]\ngives\n\\[\nT_jw_i(j+1)=\\sigma_i(j)^{-1}w_i(j).\n\\]\nThe product defining \\(c_i\\) then gives\n\\[\nT_jg_i(j+1)=g_i(j).\n\\]</p><p>Initially these traces are in \\(L^2\\). Restriction from the next larger ball is smooth on the inner sphere by Proposition \\(4\\), so the transfer identity makes every \\(g_i(j)\\) smooth.</p><p>Take its classical harmonic Dirichlet extension to \\(B_g(0,e^j)\\). The extension from \\(B_g(0,e^{j+1})\\) has the same boundary trace on the smaller ball, so Dirichlet uniqueness makes the extensions agree there. Their union is a smooth entire harmonic function \\(U_i\\).</p><p>At radius \\(e^J\\), its low projection has coordinates \\(v_i(J)\\). Those columns are independent by Proposition \\(7\\); hence the functions are independent.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every label, \\( \\lim_{j\\to\\infty}\\frac1j\\log\\|g_i(j)\\|_2=m_i. \\) The all-radius spherical bound and pointwise degree-\\(k\\) bound asserted in Proposition \\(8\\) follow.", "statement": "<p>**Growth from phase averages at every point; original Section \\(7.2\\).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the compatible entire functions and periodic means from Proposition \\(8\\) and Lemma \\(8.1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every label,\n\\[\n\\lim_{j\\to\\infty}\\frac1j\\log\\|g_i(j)\\|_2=m_i.\n\\]\nThe all-radius spherical bound and pointwise degree-\\(k\\) bound asserted in Proposition \\(8\\) follow.</p>", "proof": "<p>The graph part is orthogonal to the low part. The column bounds and \\(\\|V_j\\|=O(\\delta_j)\\) bound \\(\\|w_i(j)\\|_2\\) above and away from zero. Since \\(\\alpha_i(j)\\) range in a fixed compact positive interval,\n\\[\n\\log\\sigma_i(j)=\\log\\alpha_i(j)+O(\\delta_j)\n=d_i(j)+O(\\delta_j).\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n\\log\\|g_i(j)\\|_2\n=\\sum_{\\ell=J}^{j-1}d_i(\\ell)\n+O\\left(1+\\sum_{\\ell=J}^{j-1}\\delta_\\ell\\right).\n\\tag{7.1}\n\\]</p><p>For the Lipschitz periodic \\(\\Theta_i\\), let\n\\[\nQ_i(s)=\\int_0^s(\\Theta_i(\\sigma)-m_i)\\,d\\sigma.\n\\]\nIt is bounded because its change over \\(P_{\\mathrm{lab}}\\) is zero. Since \\(s'(t)=t^{-3/4}\\), integration by parts gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_J^T(\\Theta_i(s(t))-m_i)\\,dt\n&amp;=[t^{3/4}Q_i(s(t))]_J^T\n-\\frac34\\int_J^Tt^{-1/4}Q_i(s(t))\\,dt\\\\\n&amp;=O(T^{3/4}).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe Lipschitz bound makes the error between this integral and its unit-mesh sum at most\n\\[\nC\\sum_{j\\leq T}\\eta(j)=O(T^{1/4}).\n\\]\nOn the fixed finite spectral range, \\(b(j)\\to a\\) and smoothness of \\(d\\) imply\n\\[\nd_i(j)-\\Theta_i(s(j))\\to0\n\\]\nuniformly in \\(i\\); its Cesàro mean tends to zero. Finally,\n\\[\n\\sum_{j\\leq T}\\delta_j\n=\\sum_{j\\leq T}j^{-9/16}=O(T^{7/16}).\n\\]\nAll errors are sublinear in logarithmic time, proving\n\\[\n\\lim_{j\\to\\infty}\\frac1j\\log\\|g_i(j)\\|_2=m_i.\n\\]</p><p>Choose\n\\[\n\\max_i m_i&lt;\\gamma_*&lt;k.\n\\]\nAbsorbing finitely many initial steps,\n\\[\n\\|g_i(j)\\|_2\\leq C_i e^{\\gamma_*j}.\n\\]\nFor real \\(t\\in[j,j+1]\\), \\(U_i\\) on the radius-\\(e^t\\) sphere is restriction of the extension with outer trace \\(g_i(j+1)\\). Proposition \\(4\\) gives\n\\[\n\\|U_i(t,\\cdot)\\|_2\n\\leq\\|g_i(j+1)\\|_2\n\\leq C_i'e^{\\gamma_*t}.\n\\]\nRestriction from radius \\(e^t\\) to \\(e^j\\) also gives\n\\[\n\\|g_i(j)\\|_2\\leq\\|U_i(t,\\cdot)\\|_2.\n\\]\nTogether with the discrete limit, these bounds identify the exact spherical exponent \\(m_i\\).</p><p>The normalized divergence form of the logarithmic equation is uniformly elliptic on fixed-width cylinders in a finite sphere atlas, with uniformly bounded coefficients. The local \\(L^2\\)-to-sup estimate applied to \\(U_i\\) and \\(-U_i\\) on a cylinder of width four gives\n\\[\n\\sup_{\\omega\\in S^2}|U_i(t,\\omega)|\n\\leq\nC\\left(\\int_{t-2}^{t+2}\\|U_i(\\tau,\\cdot)\\|_2^2\\,d\\tau\\right)^{1/2}\n\\leq C_i''e^{\\gamma_*t}.\n\\]\nAbsorb the compact core into the constant. Proposition \\(2\\) gives\n\\[\nd_g(0,(r,\\omega))=r=e^t.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\gamma_*&lt;k\\),\n\\[\n|U_i(x)|\\leq C_i'''(1+d_g(0,x))^k\n\\]\neverywhere.</p>"}], "edges": [[3, 2, "part"], [4, 2, "part"], [5, 2, "part"], [6, 2, "part"], [7, 2, "part"], [8, 2, "part"], [10, 9, "part"], [11, 9, "part"], [14, 13, "part"], [15, 13, "part"], [16, 13, "part"], [18, 17, "part"], [19, 17, "part"], [20, 17, "part"], [23, 22, "part"], [24, 22, "part"], [25, 22, "part"], [27, 26, "part"], [28, 26, "part"]]});
